1
00:01:07,609 --> 00:01:08,610
Damn it.

2
00:01:44,688 --> 00:01:45,939
Stay back!

3
00:02:25,979 --> 00:02:28,439
<i>Outlaw 1, flight of two,
checking in as fragged.</i>

4
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:31,692
{\an8}<i>Red Hook 3507,
tally two hostile, one o'clock.</i>

5
00:02:31,693 --> 00:02:33,945
<i>So I got Anderton's daughter
one of those Cabbage Patch dolls</i>

6
00:02:33,946 --> 00:02:35,029
<i>for her birthday.</i>

7
00:02:35,030 --> 00:02:36,572
Can someone explain them to me?

8
00:02:36,573 --> 00:02:39,492
- What do you mean?
- Their faces. Why do they look like that?

9
00:02:39,493 --> 00:02:41,744
Their eyes are too small
and their cheeks are all swollen,

10
00:02:41,745 --> 00:02:43,121
it's like they're on steroids.

11
00:02:47,209 --> 00:02:50,294
I think they're kinda cute.
They're just chubby like babies.

12
00:02:50,295 --> 00:02:53,005
You know, if I saw a baby like that
I'd never stop screaming.

13
00:02:53,006 --> 00:02:56,843
Outlaw 2, tally. My understanding is
they're born in a cabbage patch.

14
00:02:56,844 --> 00:02:58,678
<i>You say that like it's not insane.</i>

15
00:02:58,679 --> 00:03:00,597
<i>Whatever happened to
good ole Raggedy Andy?</i>

16
00:03:01,348 --> 00:03:03,599
{\an8}- Are we gonna do this drill or not?
- <i>Uh-oh.</i>

17
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:04,767
{\an8}<i>Russians are getting impatient.</i>

18
00:03:04,768 --> 00:03:07,562
Engaging north man. Tapes on. Fight's on.

19
00:03:07,563 --> 00:03:10,065
<i>Outlaw 2, taking south man.
Turning and burning.</i>

20
00:03:20,701 --> 00:03:21,869
What you gonna do?

21
00:03:33,714 --> 00:03:36,008
Hey, you got nowhere to go, boss.
You ready to call it?

22
00:03:37,050 --> 00:03:38,385
Uh-huh. Maybe.

23
00:03:46,393 --> 00:03:48,394
Kill north man. You're dead, Nikita.

24
00:03:48,395 --> 00:03:50,396
- Dang it!
- Splash one Mig.

25
00:03:50,397 --> 00:03:51,814
Longshot, say status.

26
00:03:51,815 --> 00:03:53,191
South man is a kill, bubba.

27
00:03:53,192 --> 00:03:55,526
<i>I think we can all agree
that this was deeply embarrassing</i>

28
00:03:55,527 --> 00:03:56,903
<i>for our Soviet colleagues.</i>

29
00:03:56,904 --> 00:03:58,905
<i>Hey, you got lucky, Lieutenant.</i>

30
00:03:58,906 --> 00:04:00,199
<i>I'll see ya on the boat.</i>

31
00:04:04,244 --> 00:04:07,623
Assassin, I seem to be having
a flight controls malfunction.

32
00:04:08,457 --> 00:04:09,499
<i>What's going on?</i>

33
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,085
Right aileron's jammed.
I'm holding a full left stick.

34
00:04:13,086 --> 00:04:16,255
<i>Okay, just… Hey, hold her steady.
Lemme get a visual.</i>

35
00:04:18,550 --> 00:04:19,800
Oh, yeah, I see it.

36
00:04:19,801 --> 00:04:22,136
Can't land on mom if
I'm shaking like this.

37
00:04:22,137 --> 00:04:25,139
- LSO, you copy?
- <i>LSO copies.</i>

38
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:27,934
All right. Get my boys on the boat.
I'm gonna hang back with Boomer.

39
00:04:27,935 --> 00:04:28,936
<i>Good luck, brother.</i>

40
00:04:32,272 --> 00:04:34,398
- Damn it.
- <i>Unpucker, Boomer.</i>

41
00:04:34,399 --> 00:04:35,983
<i>Let's get you up to altitude.</i>

42
00:04:35,984 --> 00:04:38,570
No-go. Juice is 2.1. I'm nearly dry.

43
00:04:39,321 --> 00:04:40,404
Think I gotta punch out.

44
00:04:40,405 --> 00:04:42,532
<i>No. Let's think of the taxpayers.</i>

45
00:04:42,533 --> 00:04:44,325
I'm gonna try something. I just…

46
00:04:44,326 --> 00:04:46,410
<i>I want you to hold her
as steady as you can.</i>

47
00:04:46,411 --> 00:04:47,871
<i>What the hell are you doing?</i>

48
00:04:48,497 --> 00:04:50,707
- I'm gonna unjam your aileron.
- How?

49
00:04:51,291 --> 00:04:54,169
<i>Don't worry.
Just drop your gear, fly your needles.</i>

50
00:04:59,424 --> 00:05:01,050
It's too unstable.

51
00:05:01,051 --> 00:05:03,178
I know. This is kind of crazy.
Just go with me.

52
00:05:05,806 --> 00:05:07,307
You're gonna get us both killed.

53
00:05:19,611 --> 00:05:22,113
Oh, shit. Come on!

54
00:05:22,114 --> 00:05:24,657
It's okay. That's all right.
I'm a professional.

55
00:05:24,658 --> 00:05:26,200
<i>It's no-go.</i>

56
00:05:26,201 --> 00:05:28,661
Wave off, 382. You're gonna hit the tower.

57
00:05:28,662 --> 00:05:30,079
He's right. You gotta abort.

58
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:32,790
<i>You just focus on Meatball. Lineup AOA.</i>

59
00:05:32,791 --> 00:05:35,043
Now, you're gonna feel a little pressure.

60
00:05:49,933 --> 00:05:52,643
Okay, all right. I owe you one, buddy.

61
00:05:52,644 --> 00:05:53,853
I owe you more.

62
00:05:53,854 --> 00:05:55,313
You just made me employee of the month.

63
00:05:55,314 --> 00:05:58,025
<i>They're gonna let me
park my plane next to the entrance.</i>

64
00:05:58,525 --> 00:06:00,610
<i>Let me say to the hostage families,</i>

65
00:06:00,611 --> 00:06:01,694
<i>we have not given up.</i>

66
00:06:01,695 --> 00:06:02,945
<i>We never will.</i>

67
00:06:02,946 --> 00:06:05,656
<i>And I promise you we'll use
every legitimate means</i>

68
00:06:05,657 --> 00:06:09,410
<i>to free your loved ones from captivity.
But I must also…</i>

69
00:06:09,411 --> 00:06:12,496
- Hey, what'd he say?
- He said I'm gonna live another 75 years.

70
00:06:12,497 --> 00:06:15,125
Oh, come on. Look, is that new stuff
he's got you on doing anything?

71
00:06:15,626 --> 00:06:17,251
I told you I'm cured.

72
00:06:17,252 --> 00:06:18,420
- Oh, you're cured?
- Excuse me?

73
00:06:19,087 --> 00:06:21,964
Rules are you gotta be in a wheelchair
until you leave the hospital.

74
00:06:21,965 --> 00:06:24,592
- Really?
- Come on. No, no, no. Let's not make a--

75
00:06:24,593 --> 00:06:27,053
Dad, you don't have to protect me
from this stuff.

76
00:06:27,054 --> 00:06:28,930
I'm pretty good with stressful things.

77
00:06:28,931 --> 00:06:32,433
Oh, Lord.
Well, they're gonna try a new medicine.

78
00:06:32,434 --> 00:06:34,394
It's worked on rats,
so it should be good for me.

79
00:06:36,939 --> 00:06:39,607
Hey, you wanna go to the Turf tonight?
Grab a steak or something?

80
00:06:39,608 --> 00:06:42,401
- I thought you were meetin' your friends?
- You're my friends.

81
00:06:42,402 --> 00:06:43,904
Well, thank you very much.

82
00:06:44,738 --> 00:06:48,241
But if you wanna cheer me up,
then go drink with your pals.

83
00:06:48,242 --> 00:06:50,869
At least pretend you haven't
given up so much for me.

84
00:06:51,703 --> 00:06:55,498
Besides,
I'm going out with that girl tonight.

85
00:06:55,499 --> 00:06:56,666
Oh, nice.

86
00:06:56,667 --> 00:06:57,959
Dinner and a funeral.

87
00:06:57,960 --> 00:06:59,085
You're a dick.

88
00:06:59,086 --> 00:07:01,296
GASLAMP QUARTER

89
00:07:03,340 --> 00:07:05,550
Oh, please. Don't let Gorbachev fool you.

90
00:07:05,551 --> 00:07:08,135
Right, well, I don't know if
Gorbachev is the problem.

91
00:07:08,136 --> 00:07:09,554
- It seems to me like…
- Oh, no.

92
00:07:09,555 --> 00:07:12,390
- …he is trying to make some reforms.
- The guy came up through the Politburo.

93
00:07:12,391 --> 00:07:14,350
He's a dyed-in-the-wool Communist.

94
00:07:14,351 --> 00:07:16,686
- Regardless of what he calls himself.
- Here comes Captain America.

95
00:07:16,687 --> 00:07:18,187
- That's right.
- Honestly. I don't see

96
00:07:18,188 --> 00:07:20,314
the big difference between
capitalism and communism.

97
00:07:20,315 --> 00:07:22,859
- You what? The whole Soviet system…
- Why would you poke the bear, man?

98
00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:25,194
- …flies in the face of human nature.
- Great, now he's not gonna shut up.

99
00:07:25,195 --> 00:07:26,904
You can't reform something

100
00:07:26,905 --> 00:07:28,990
- that's fundamentally broken.
- I didn't sign up for this course.

101
00:07:28,991 --> 00:07:31,158
- No, you didn't.
- You didn't sign up for this course.

102
00:07:31,159 --> 00:07:33,452
- Lenin, Stalin, you know…
- Didn't we just go out?

103
00:07:33,453 --> 00:07:35,997
These guys would pretend that
we're living in some-- Hi, there.

104
00:07:35,998 --> 00:07:38,749
We're living in some,
you know, big, happy collective.

105
00:07:38,750 --> 00:07:42,253
You know, but for some reason,
that collective is always at the cost of

106
00:07:42,254 --> 00:07:43,754
individual freedoms.

107
00:07:43,755 --> 00:07:46,841
- Watch out!
- Sorry. Hi. You drive safe tonight, sir.

108
00:07:46,842 --> 00:07:48,092
Okay. Take me, for example.

109
00:07:48,093 --> 00:07:51,138
I think we can all agree
that I'm the best pilot in the 3rd Fleet.

110
00:07:51,847 --> 00:07:53,431
Boomer, I know you can.

111
00:07:53,432 --> 00:07:55,725
- What? Wh… No shame, this guy!
- Save me, Troy.

112
00:07:55,726 --> 00:07:58,477
- I don't have any shame. Nor should I.
- Save me.

113
00:07:58,478 --> 00:08:00,605
Right, because in America,
we celebrate being the best.

114
00:08:00,606 --> 00:08:02,273
- Yep.
- We reward greatness.

115
00:08:02,274 --> 00:08:04,192
You know what happens
over there if you're great?

116
00:08:04,193 --> 00:08:05,359
What's that? Tell them.

117
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:07,695
Nothing. You're just another comrade.

118
00:08:07,696 --> 00:08:09,864
- That's not a society…
- You guys sucking up to him?

119
00:08:09,865 --> 00:08:11,157
- …that's a prison.
- What?

120
00:08:11,158 --> 00:08:14,202
And anyone who doesn't fight to break out
of that, well, then they're, you know…

121
00:08:14,203 --> 00:08:15,661
- They're part of the problem.
- Anyone?

122
00:08:15,662 --> 00:08:17,580
Even the poor civs
who just happen to be born there?

123
00:08:17,581 --> 00:08:19,290
Silence is complicity.

124
00:08:19,291 --> 00:08:21,668
- Top guns! Yeah. What's up?
- Hey, buddy.

125
00:08:22,836 --> 00:08:25,463
You know, it's times like this
I am so glad they made that movie.

126
00:08:25,464 --> 00:08:27,299
- Uh-huh. What's up?
- Gentlemen.

127
00:08:28,509 --> 00:08:29,676
{\an8}Commander.

128
00:08:30,469 --> 00:08:31,470
Everything okay, sir?

129
00:08:32,136 --> 00:08:33,931
Speak privately for a moment?

130
00:08:35,015 --> 00:08:36,225
Just Lieutenant Kelly.

131
00:08:37,476 --> 00:08:39,520
I've been briefed on your stunt today.

132
00:08:40,270 --> 00:08:42,605
Okay, yeah. I saw
an opportunity to help a wingman

133
00:08:42,606 --> 00:08:44,315
- and save an aircraft, so--
- Easy, Kelly.

134
00:08:44,316 --> 00:08:45,776
I'm not here to tear you down.

135
00:08:46,985 --> 00:08:48,362
You ever heard of Big Bird?

136
00:08:49,530 --> 00:08:51,365
Not the one from <i>Sesame Street</i>.

137
00:08:52,616 --> 00:08:53,617
Then no.

138
00:08:54,326 --> 00:08:55,619
Operation Big Bird.

139
00:08:56,328 --> 00:09:00,122
Since '71, the NRO has been
sending up recon satellites

140
00:09:00,123 --> 00:09:06,588
to keep track of what the Russians are
doing in the remote regions of USSR.

141
00:09:07,589 --> 00:09:09,966
Problem with that is
resolution is limited.

142
00:09:09,967 --> 00:09:13,262
So occasionally, we have to
send someone out for a closer look.

143
00:09:13,804 --> 00:09:14,888
You need a spy plane.

144
00:09:16,974 --> 00:09:18,475
You want me to fly a spy plane.

145
00:09:18,976 --> 00:09:22,353
You ship out to Ramstein Air Base
in Germany, 0400 tomorrow.

146
00:09:22,354 --> 00:09:23,396
I'll brief you there.

147
00:09:23,397 --> 00:09:26,190
And it's TS,
so don't tell anyone you're leaving.

148
00:09:26,191 --> 00:09:28,359
Thank you, sir.
For the opportunity. Thank you.

149
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:30,612
No, thank you. For earning it.

150
00:09:44,459 --> 00:09:46,711
Last week, we received imagery suggesting

151
00:09:46,712 --> 00:09:50,548
that the Russians are building
missile silos northeast of Nyandoma.

152
00:09:50,549 --> 00:09:55,052
That places our area of ops
1,200 miles inside the Soviet border.

153
00:09:55,053 --> 00:09:57,181
That's 30 minutes in and 30 minutes out.

154
00:09:57,973 --> 00:10:01,601
The site is flanked by four SAM sites
as far as we know.

155
00:10:01,602 --> 00:10:05,313
And the nearest Russian airstrip is
at Os'mino, 200 miles east.

156
00:10:05,314 --> 00:10:09,860
All this suggests that the Russians are
not using this site to make nesting dolls.

157
00:10:10,736 --> 00:10:14,071
You'll fly in at 80,000 feet,
above the SAM ceiling.

158
00:10:14,072 --> 00:10:16,033
You'll get your shots and you'll <i>vámonos</i>.

159
00:10:18,827 --> 00:10:20,287
You're the only one here, Kelly.

160
00:10:21,330 --> 00:10:22,914
You said 80,000 feet, sir.

161
00:10:22,915 --> 00:10:25,458
Doesn't that exceed
the max altitude of a U-2?

162
00:10:25,459 --> 00:10:28,420
That's correct.
But you won't be flying the U-2.

163
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:37,720
The SR-71 Blackbird.

164
00:10:37,721 --> 00:10:43,184
Max speed Mach 3 plus,
ceiling 85,000 feet.

165
00:10:43,185 --> 00:10:45,729
Fastest, highest flying aircraft
in the world.

166
00:10:47,356 --> 00:10:48,398
She's a beauty.

167
00:10:49,107 --> 00:10:50,650
She's a beast.

168
00:10:50,651 --> 00:10:51,860
She can outrun a missile.

169
00:10:52,694 --> 00:10:54,153
You'll be in a full pressure suit.

170
00:10:54,154 --> 00:10:56,113
But that won't stop you
from getting shredded

171
00:10:56,114 --> 00:10:58,534
should you decide to eject above Mach 1.

172
00:10:59,743 --> 00:11:02,704
Correct me if I'm wrong, sir,
but isn't this an Air Force op?

173
00:11:03,830 --> 00:11:06,874
It's a black op.
You're flying for the CIA.

174
00:11:06,875 --> 00:11:09,210
- You're decommissioning me.
- Temporarily.

175
00:11:09,211 --> 00:11:11,337
Your training with the cameras
used on the Blackbird

176
00:11:11,338 --> 00:11:13,256
makes you uniquely qualified.

177
00:11:13,257 --> 00:11:15,383
You'll be second seat
to an Air Force flyer.

178
00:11:15,384 --> 00:11:17,094
Wait. I'm RIO? I'm not flying?

179
00:11:17,678 --> 00:11:18,762
Not this time, squid.

180
00:11:20,097 --> 00:11:21,973
Major Sanders. Lieutenant Kelly.

181
00:11:21,974 --> 00:11:24,685
The major here's flown 23 sorties
on the Blackbird.

182
00:11:25,853 --> 00:11:27,563
- Nice to meet you.
- Uh-huh.

183
00:11:28,230 --> 00:11:30,398
You ever come under fire in this?

184
00:11:30,399 --> 00:11:32,359
Wouldn't have been doing my job if I had.

185
00:11:33,151 --> 00:11:37,114
This isn't a Navy tac-air beer run.
Recon requires less brawn, more brains.

186
00:11:38,198 --> 00:11:40,033
I can see that. Yeah.

187
00:11:42,870 --> 00:11:45,121
You just keep your eyes on the radar
and let me do the rest.

188
00:11:45,122 --> 00:11:47,206
Got it. Okay, I'm picking up
a bogey with a--

189
00:11:47,207 --> 00:11:49,542
just a big old pile of cheek bones
and eyebrows at my 12.

190
00:11:49,543 --> 00:11:51,961
You can fang each
other all you want on the ground here.

191
00:11:51,962 --> 00:11:55,214
But up there, you are one or you are dead.
Remember that.

192
00:11:55,215 --> 00:11:57,383
Understood, sir.
Sorry I made fun of your face.

193
00:11:57,384 --> 00:11:59,720
You just don't see 'em like that
outside of nightmares.

194
00:12:03,348 --> 00:12:04,974
Jesus, Dad.

195
00:12:04,975 --> 00:12:06,767
<i>Hell, I'm fine. I'm fine.</i>

196
00:12:06,768 --> 00:12:09,187
I thought you didn't come home
'cause you got lucky.

197
00:12:09,188 --> 00:12:11,315
Well, I might've. We'll see.

198
00:12:12,232 --> 00:12:13,566
Should I be worried?

199
00:12:13,567 --> 00:12:15,944
<i>No, no, no, no. It's a--
It's a walk in the park.</i>

200
00:12:16,695 --> 00:12:18,696
Which park? Libya?

201
00:12:18,697 --> 00:12:19,823
<i>Iran?</i>

202
00:12:20,365 --> 00:12:22,284
That… Well, a classified park actually.

203
00:12:23,952 --> 00:12:26,205
- Well, don't get yourself in any tr--
- <i>No, no. Never do.</i>

204
00:12:27,039 --> 00:12:28,373
<i>Never do. I gotta go.</i>

205
00:12:29,750 --> 00:12:31,460
I… I love you, Pop.

206
00:12:33,754 --> 00:12:34,755
Love you too, pal.

207
00:13:17,297 --> 00:13:18,548
Okay.

208
00:13:18,549 --> 00:13:20,091
You're gonna wanna put those in your boot.

209
00:13:20,092 --> 00:13:21,175
Sorry?

210
00:13:21,176 --> 00:13:22,928
The chain can choke you
if you have to eject.

211
00:13:23,846 --> 00:13:24,972
Any zoomie would know that.

212
00:13:25,681 --> 00:13:27,975
Oh, yeah? Zoomies know that?

213
00:13:36,817 --> 00:13:38,360
<i>Tank pressure check.</i>

214
00:13:39,111 --> 00:13:42,906
<i>She's at 1.3 PSI. Setting IGV to lockout.</i>

215
00:13:46,285 --> 00:13:49,036
<i>Habu 43, checklist complete?</i>

216
00:13:49,037 --> 00:13:51,330
Affirmative. IGV normal.

217
00:13:51,331 --> 00:13:53,291
OBC camera OP checks good.

218
00:13:53,292 --> 00:13:58,755
<i>Habu 43, wind 265 at five.
Cleared for takeoff, runway 26.</i>

219
00:14:19,776 --> 00:14:22,654
So, Sanders, are you married? Kids?

220
00:14:23,238 --> 00:14:25,157
Not a priority till I make colonel.

221
00:14:25,866 --> 00:14:29,286
Same. You think that's why they chose us?

222
00:14:29,870 --> 00:14:31,038
No ties?

223
00:14:31,997 --> 00:14:33,498
You know, we don't have to talk.

224
00:14:36,793 --> 00:14:39,671
You do realize if your dream comes true,
what that'll make you?

225
00:14:41,298 --> 00:14:42,299
What's that?

226
00:14:42,883 --> 00:14:44,301
Colonel Sanders.

227
00:14:47,721 --> 00:14:49,264
No one's ever pointed that out?

228
00:14:51,975 --> 00:14:53,477
1,700 knots.

229
00:14:54,353 --> 00:14:55,854
And 11 herbs and spices.

230
00:15:16,875 --> 00:15:20,295
It's all just one big chunk of land
from up here.

231
00:15:23,632 --> 00:15:25,175
Flight level eight-zero-zero.

232
00:15:26,009 --> 00:15:27,301
<i>Copy that.</i>

233
00:15:27,302 --> 00:15:30,263
<i>Be advised, you are now
crossing Soviet airspace,</i>

234
00:15:30,264 --> 00:15:32,099
<i>512 miles from target.</i>

235
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:37,521
OBC camera on. Ready.

236
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:42,901
How are we looking?

237
00:15:43,694 --> 00:15:47,614
Heavy soup over the target.
Doubt we'll get anything clean.

238
00:15:48,991 --> 00:15:51,785
Base, Habu. We've got poor vis.
Please advise.

239
00:15:53,745 --> 00:15:56,248
<i>Habu,
descend to flight level four-five-zero.</i>

240
00:15:57,666 --> 00:15:58,959
First time for everything.

241
00:16:00,419 --> 00:16:01,962
<i>Copy. Four-five-zero.</i>

242
00:16:12,389 --> 00:16:13,557
There we go.

243
00:16:14,391 --> 00:16:17,144
Film is moving. We got eyes on target.

244
00:16:21,064 --> 00:16:24,067
<i>Bravo, zulu. Get back on altitude and RTB.</i>

245
00:16:41,668 --> 00:16:43,462
Bogey one-nine-zero-ten.

246
00:16:44,963 --> 00:16:46,006
Damn it.

247
00:16:50,636 --> 00:16:54,765
Tally one Mig, five o'clock.
Jamming engaged and working.

248
00:17:01,897 --> 00:17:04,066
Why are you banking? We'll lose airspeed.

249
00:17:04,608 --> 00:17:05,942
I've got this.

250
00:17:05,943 --> 00:17:07,444
No, you don't. He'll gain on us.

251
00:17:10,696 --> 00:17:14,409
See, Kelly? This is why you're
a trunk monkey and I'm a--

252
00:17:17,246 --> 00:17:21,124
Sanders? Sanders, do you copy?

253
00:17:26,463 --> 00:17:29,006
Habu 43, defensive. Scram east.

254
00:17:29,007 --> 00:17:31,425
100 miles west of Pechora.

255
00:17:31,426 --> 00:17:33,887
Pilot is unresponsive
and I've lost all flight control.

256
00:17:39,309 --> 00:17:40,227
Come on.

257
00:18:01,540 --> 00:18:02,749
Right engine is bent.

258
00:18:07,421 --> 00:18:08,629
Mayday, mayday, mayday.

259
00:18:08,630 --> 00:18:13,759
Habu 43 marking latitude 65.18,
longitude 72.10.

260
00:18:13,760 --> 00:18:14,969
I'm gonna have to punch out.

261
00:18:14,970 --> 00:18:17,346
<i>You cannot eject at that speed, Assassin!</i>

262
00:18:17,347 --> 00:18:18,640
I know, I know.

263
00:18:24,646 --> 00:18:26,398
That's it. Get up here.

264
00:19:51,525 --> 00:19:52,526
Okay.

265
00:20:33,483 --> 00:20:36,403
What is this? Hey!

266
00:20:40,490 --> 00:20:41,575
Good. You have not died.

267
00:20:42,993 --> 00:20:44,411
Who are you? Where am I?

268
00:20:45,954 --> 00:20:48,122
Well, my name is Nikolai Ustinov.

269
00:20:48,123 --> 00:20:49,373
But you should call me Nick.

270
00:20:49,374 --> 00:20:51,460
And you are in my home.

271
00:20:52,252 --> 00:20:54,337
Welcome. It is an honor.

272
00:20:54,338 --> 00:20:55,672
Why am I tied to the bed?

273
00:20:56,965 --> 00:21:00,177
I tied you so that you would not move
during the surgery.

274
00:21:01,094 --> 00:21:02,220
What do you mean surgery?

275
00:21:02,221 --> 00:21:05,515
The stick in your leg.
I removed it and repaired an artery.

276
00:21:05,516 --> 00:21:07,099
There was a great deal of blood.

277
00:21:07,100 --> 00:21:08,935
You screamed a lot, but that is my fault

278
00:21:08,936 --> 00:21:10,979
because I do not have proper anesthesia.

279
00:21:11,813 --> 00:21:13,232
Can y… can you untie me now?

280
00:21:14,066 --> 00:21:16,818
Of course. Soon, yes.

281
00:21:17,778 --> 00:21:22,574
But I need you to rest now
so that the healing can continue. Uh-huh.

282
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:27,453
Oh, okay. What is… what is with the…
all the American stuff?

283
00:21:27,454 --> 00:21:33,126
All the… Well, this may shock you,
but I admire your country very much.

284
00:21:33,877 --> 00:21:36,712
Your democracy and your culture.

285
00:21:36,713 --> 00:21:37,797
And your food.

286
00:21:37,798 --> 00:21:40,551
I have never had McDonald's,
but I suspect I would love it.

287
00:21:41,134 --> 00:21:42,845
You could almost say I am obsessed.

288
00:21:44,346 --> 00:21:45,638
Oh, is that right?

289
00:21:45,639 --> 00:21:48,684
My dream is to someday live in America.

290
00:21:49,601 --> 00:21:53,272
Drive from sea to shining sea
in a recreational vehicle.

291
00:21:54,273 --> 00:21:56,567
- Fun.
- I don't know yet…

292
00:21:59,027 --> 00:22:00,611
what I would do for work.

293
00:22:00,612 --> 00:22:02,905
But I know that there,
anything is possible.

294
00:22:02,906 --> 00:22:03,907
Yeah.

295
00:22:07,286 --> 00:22:09,204
I have something I want to show you.

296
00:22:22,176 --> 00:22:26,221
This is very special,
but also quite illegal.

297
00:22:28,223 --> 00:22:30,642
A man could go to jail for having this.

298
00:22:31,602 --> 00:22:33,936
I have watched it over 100 times.

299
00:22:33,937 --> 00:22:36,899
And each time,
I imagine I am the man in the movie.

300
00:22:37,900 --> 00:22:40,152
Doing the things that he does.

301
00:22:42,362 --> 00:22:45,364
You see, we don't get
many American films here,

302
00:22:45,365 --> 00:22:48,368
but last year I came upon this bootleg…

303
00:22:49,870 --> 00:22:51,163
of <i>Top Gun</i>.

304
00:22:53,749 --> 00:22:57,294
It confirmed for me
all of my beliefs about your people.

305
00:22:58,086 --> 00:23:00,214
And then, suddenly one day, here you are.

306
00:23:00,797 --> 00:23:03,091
Falling from the sky like providence.

307
00:23:03,926 --> 00:23:05,511
- Listen, Nikolai. I'm--
- Stop!

308
00:23:08,430 --> 00:23:10,057
Nick. Call me Nick.

309
00:23:10,682 --> 00:23:12,975
Nick. I'm Troy.

310
00:23:12,976 --> 00:23:16,688
- Troy, nice to meet you.
- I appreciate you patching me up, Nick.

311
00:23:17,940 --> 00:23:19,690
But, as you can imagine,
I really need to get going.

312
00:23:19,691 --> 00:23:21,693
I am behind enemy lines.

313
00:23:22,528 --> 00:23:24,904
Not that… Not that you're an enemy. Right?

314
00:23:24,905 --> 00:23:27,115
Just the opposite, Troy.
I am your biggest fan.

315
00:23:28,200 --> 00:23:29,992
Good. That's good.

316
00:23:29,993 --> 00:23:32,328
So, you'll understand why
I need to get across the border.

317
00:23:32,329 --> 00:23:34,455
Of course. The nearest border is Finland.

318
00:23:34,456 --> 00:23:35,831
How far is that?

319
00:23:35,832 --> 00:23:37,041
2,000 kilometers.

320
00:23:37,042 --> 00:23:38,752
Well, shit.

321
00:23:48,387 --> 00:23:49,805
It's been two years.

322
00:23:50,639 --> 00:23:55,477
Why didn't you do this
when Chernenko died?

323
00:23:58,480 --> 00:24:00,940
It gives me no pleasure
to hang the portrait

324
00:24:00,941 --> 00:24:02,985
of a man who is destroying my country.

325
00:24:03,694 --> 00:24:06,697
Gorbachev would argue he is saving it.

326
00:24:09,491 --> 00:24:12,660
I still remember
when Russia was an empire.

327
00:24:12,661 --> 00:24:15,455
Half the world fell within our influence.
Look at us now.

328
00:24:16,164 --> 00:24:19,667
Losing a bloodless war
to a soulless superpower.

329
00:24:19,668 --> 00:24:24,673
And this bald fool wants us to be
closer to our enemy.

330
00:24:26,508 --> 00:24:27,550
What is it?

331
00:24:27,551 --> 00:24:30,178
An American spy plane shot down
over Khorovaya.

332
00:24:30,179 --> 00:24:32,431
They believe one of the pilots ejected.

333
00:24:33,765 --> 00:24:35,058
Have they secured the crash site?

334
00:24:36,768 --> 00:24:39,229
Not yet.
General Korzhev is leading the search.

335
00:24:42,900 --> 00:24:43,901
Not anymore.

336
00:24:53,744 --> 00:24:56,246
<i>Goose! Goose, hold on!</i>

337
00:25:01,502 --> 00:25:02,418
<i>Oh, God.</i>

338
00:25:02,419 --> 00:25:05,129
Goose and Maverick
were more than copilots.

339
00:25:05,130 --> 00:25:06,631
They were best friends.

340
00:25:06,632 --> 00:25:07,925
Yes, they were.

341
00:25:10,427 --> 00:25:13,137
This is where the man
filming the movie goes to the toilet.

342
00:25:13,138 --> 00:25:14,515
Do you recall what happens here?

343
00:25:15,140 --> 00:25:17,351
I do not. I'm so sorry.

344
00:25:19,478 --> 00:25:21,312
No problem. How is your meat?

345
00:25:21,313 --> 00:25:24,357
Oh, well, this is very, very hard.

346
00:25:24,358 --> 00:25:25,692
But at least it's sour. So…

347
00:25:26,818 --> 00:25:30,404
Yeah, in the winter,
it is difficult to find fresh bear.

348
00:25:30,405 --> 00:25:31,447
I'm eating bear?

349
00:25:31,448 --> 00:25:33,533
Yeah, I have 40 kilos of it
in my root cellar.

350
00:25:33,534 --> 00:25:34,743
Eat as much as you like.

351
00:25:35,244 --> 00:25:36,911
Thank you. What is this?

352
00:25:36,912 --> 00:25:39,247
Samogon. I make it from beets.

353
00:25:39,248 --> 00:25:40,374
In a barrel.

354
00:25:41,208 --> 00:25:42,458
That doesn't taste like beets.

355
00:25:42,459 --> 00:25:44,127
Oh, thank you very much.

356
00:25:45,879 --> 00:25:51,802
I noticed on your flight suit
your call sign is Assassin.

357
00:25:53,303 --> 00:25:55,097
May I ask the origin of this name?

358
00:25:56,014 --> 00:25:58,016
That's a story that only me
and my friends know.

359
00:25:59,726 --> 00:26:00,894
Oh, I see.

360
00:26:03,480 --> 00:26:08,986
I hope someday you and I
will be such friends.

361
00:26:10,487 --> 00:26:14,282
Definitely. I should probably be going.

362
00:26:14,283 --> 00:26:16,909
If you can just untie me,
I'll be on my way.

363
00:26:16,910 --> 00:26:18,828
But if I untie you, you will leave.

364
00:26:18,829 --> 00:26:21,039
Yes. That's what I just said.

365
00:26:21,540 --> 00:26:23,834
I don't think you understand
your situation.

366
00:26:24,585 --> 00:26:27,378
Thousands of kilometers
stand between you and safety.

367
00:26:27,379 --> 00:26:29,840
The Red Army will already
be looking for you.

368
00:26:30,340 --> 00:26:33,468
And when they find you,
they will give you to the KGB.

369
00:26:34,303 --> 00:26:37,180
And that is a fate I would wish on no one.

370
00:26:37,181 --> 00:26:40,225
Believe me, I am your safe haven.

371
00:26:40,726 --> 00:26:42,518
I can't stay here.

372
00:26:42,519 --> 00:26:44,312
Just until the Cold War dies down.

373
00:26:44,313 --> 00:26:46,731
The Cold War has been
going on for 40 years.

374
00:26:46,732 --> 00:26:48,567
Exactly. How much longer can it last?

375
00:26:50,694 --> 00:26:55,281
Okay. Okay. You know what,
you're probably right.

376
00:26:55,282 --> 00:26:58,326
Look, I'm… It would be insane to
try to do this in my condition.

377
00:26:58,327 --> 00:26:59,911
And I would be insane to let you go.

378
00:26:59,912 --> 00:27:01,204
Right. Look, okay.

379
00:27:01,205 --> 00:27:03,749
So, here's what I'll…
I'll stay until it's safe to go.

380
00:27:04,333 --> 00:27:05,542
- Excellent.
- Good.

381
00:27:06,210 --> 00:27:08,586
Are you gonna…
Are you gonna keep me tied up until then?

382
00:27:08,587 --> 00:27:12,840
Of course not. I just needed to know
that we can trust each other, yeah?

383
00:27:12,841 --> 00:27:16,512
I trust you completely. Nick, thank you.

384
00:27:17,012 --> 00:27:18,387
- Yeah, no problem.
- Yeah.

385
00:27:18,388 --> 00:27:19,932
We're going to have a wonderful time.

386
00:27:20,474 --> 00:27:21,308
That's good.

387
00:27:23,936 --> 00:27:24,978
That's better. Okay.

388
00:27:28,649 --> 00:27:29,816
I'm sorry, Nick.

389
00:27:31,944 --> 00:27:33,028
Please stay.

390
00:27:49,086 --> 00:27:50,712
How are you so spry?

391
00:27:53,549 --> 00:27:55,676
Very effective guillotine.

392
00:28:01,723 --> 00:28:04,058
You are damaging yourself.
Let us stop now.

393
00:28:04,059 --> 00:28:05,310
Okay. You win.

394
00:28:08,856 --> 00:28:09,898
Wonderful.

395
00:28:24,413 --> 00:28:25,789
Find the keys. Keys.

396
00:28:36,258 --> 00:28:38,593
Hey! Come back inside, my friend.

397
00:28:38,594 --> 00:28:39,595
I will make tea.

398
00:30:53,937 --> 00:30:56,772
Please connect me to
the US Embassy in Moscow. Quickly.

399
00:30:56,773 --> 00:30:57,608
Qui--

400
00:31:01,653 --> 00:31:05,240
This is for you. Practicing English.

401
00:31:06,116 --> 00:31:08,035
Okay. No, no, no. Hey, hey, hey.

402
00:31:09,453 --> 00:31:10,537
I'm leaving. Okay? I--

403
00:31:11,205 --> 00:31:13,165
Okay. Listen to me. It's fine. It's--

404
00:31:21,882 --> 00:31:22,966
Don't--

405
00:32:52,514 --> 00:32:53,598
All right.

406
00:32:53,599 --> 00:32:54,433
Nikolai!

407
00:32:55,184 --> 00:32:56,894
What have you done?

408
00:32:59,313 --> 00:33:00,647
This should settle their tab.

409
00:33:06,737 --> 00:33:10,740
Who are you?
Where did you learn to fight like that?

410
00:33:10,741 --> 00:33:13,868
I'm sure you would have held your own
if not for your injury.

411
00:33:13,869 --> 00:33:16,622
American combat training
is far superior to ours.

412
00:33:17,331 --> 00:33:19,499
"Ours"? You were military?

413
00:33:20,125 --> 00:33:21,293
It doesn't matter.

414
00:33:22,336 --> 00:33:24,963
Well, it matters to me.
I wanna know who I'm sitting here with.

415
00:33:29,801 --> 00:33:31,762
Years ago, I was a state security officer.

416
00:33:32,596 --> 00:33:36,058
Hold on. You're KGB?
What do you do for them?

417
00:33:36,850 --> 00:33:38,143
Nothing now.

418
00:33:38,727 --> 00:33:42,105
I led a…
a team that carried out covert ops.

419
00:33:42,648 --> 00:33:45,733
Counterintelligence. Interrogation.

420
00:33:45,734 --> 00:33:47,109
Oh, so you're a monster.

421
00:33:47,110 --> 00:33:49,571
I believed I was acting
in the interest of my country.

422
00:33:51,073 --> 00:33:51,989
Right.

423
00:33:51,990 --> 00:33:55,451
But when my daughter grew old enough
to ask me what I did when I went to work,

424
00:33:55,452 --> 00:33:58,664
I was too ashamed to tell her.

425
00:33:59,748 --> 00:34:02,041
You see, when a man does something
for long enough,

426
00:34:02,042 --> 00:34:03,377
it stops being what he does.

427
00:34:04,878 --> 00:34:06,296
It becomes who he is.

428
00:34:09,882 --> 00:34:13,386
So I'm supposed to buy that you
just washed your hands of all this,

429
00:34:13,387 --> 00:34:15,848
moved up here
and became Grizzly Adams? Is that right?

430
00:34:16,556 --> 00:34:20,560
I had to put myself
as far from the regime as I could.

431
00:34:20,561 --> 00:34:23,354
And I need to put myself
as far from you as I can.

432
00:34:23,355 --> 00:34:26,608
Listen to me. If I were still
that monster, why would I take you in?

433
00:34:27,484 --> 00:34:29,777
Why would I nurse you
and fight those men at the bar?

434
00:34:30,445 --> 00:34:32,822
I've aided an enemy of the Republic.

435
00:34:33,949 --> 00:34:35,617
That is punishable by death.

436
00:34:40,246 --> 00:34:42,165
I appreciate you helping me out.

437
00:34:43,792 --> 00:34:46,502
- I'm sorry I set you on fire.
- You were trying to get home.

438
00:34:46,503 --> 00:34:47,669
Lot of good it did me.

439
00:34:47,670 --> 00:34:49,840
I couldn't even get a call
through to the embassy.

440
00:34:51,425 --> 00:34:55,094
Even if you had, you'd only be
bringing state security to our door.

441
00:34:55,929 --> 00:34:58,182
The government monitors all calls
to foreign embassies.

442
00:35:00,058 --> 00:35:02,101
- What if I called on this?
- That's a relic.

443
00:35:02,102 --> 00:35:04,563
It's a 30 kilometer range on a good day.

444
00:35:05,189 --> 00:35:08,233
The only way is
if you had access to an Altai.

445
00:35:09,526 --> 00:35:12,320
- A what?
- It's a mobile radiotelephone.

446
00:35:12,321 --> 00:35:14,113
They can't be traced.
They're not monitored.

447
00:35:14,114 --> 00:35:15,865
Okay. How do I get one of those?

448
00:35:15,866 --> 00:35:18,868
You don't. They're only available
to high-placed government officials.

449
00:35:18,869 --> 00:35:21,078
And I'm guessing you don't have
a lot of those out here?

450
00:35:21,079 --> 00:35:21,996
No. Not here.

451
00:35:21,997 --> 00:35:24,832
- Out in Lake… Lake Vozhe, though.
- Lake Vozhe?

452
00:35:24,833 --> 00:35:28,336
Many of the members of
the Supreme Soviet have dachas there.

453
00:35:28,337 --> 00:35:29,837
Is that close?

454
00:35:29,838 --> 00:35:33,592
It's about
halfway between here and Moscow.

455
00:35:34,468 --> 00:35:37,094
Perfect! We're gonna steal an Altai,

456
00:35:37,095 --> 00:35:39,806
I'm gonna call the embassy and
they're gonna get me the hell out of here.

457
00:35:41,391 --> 00:35:42,517
What?

458
00:35:42,518 --> 00:35:44,728
You would be rescued, but I…

459
00:35:46,021 --> 00:35:47,022
Right.

460
00:35:48,357 --> 00:35:49,900
What if I take you with me to the States?

461
00:35:50,734 --> 00:35:52,568
Soviets cannot emigrate, Troy.

462
00:35:52,569 --> 00:35:54,946
No, not normally.
But I'm a shot-down pilot.

463
00:35:54,947 --> 00:35:57,698
I'm gonna be a national hero. They'll
probably give me the Medal of Honor.

464
00:35:57,699 --> 00:35:59,575
There's no way the embassy's gonna
say no to me, Nick.

465
00:35:59,576 --> 00:36:00,952
Unfortunately, it's not so simple.

466
00:36:00,953 --> 00:36:03,329
I have a daughter in Moscow.
I cannot leave without her.

467
00:36:03,330 --> 00:36:04,288
We'll take her along.

468
00:36:04,289 --> 00:36:09,627
She wouldn't come. She does not
believe that I am a changed man.

469
00:36:09,628 --> 00:36:11,338
Talk to her. Make her believe it.

470
00:36:11,922 --> 00:36:18,595
Russians are not as comfortable
sharing their feelings as your people.

471
00:36:19,179 --> 00:36:22,348
You said that your dream
was to become an American.

472
00:36:22,349 --> 00:36:24,810
Start acting like an American.

473
00:36:25,978 --> 00:36:27,271
Nick, will you help me?

474
00:36:30,566 --> 00:36:35,319
This is very tempting,
but it is also very dangerous.

475
00:36:35,320 --> 00:36:38,448
Hey. Isn't it about high time that we

476
00:36:39,783 --> 00:36:41,535
"ride into the danger zone"?

477
00:36:43,871 --> 00:36:44,955
Kenny Loggins.

478
00:36:46,540 --> 00:36:47,665
I understand this joke.

479
00:36:47,666 --> 00:36:48,876
We got a deal?

480
00:36:51,128 --> 00:36:53,796
Let me finish my drink first
before I decide.

481
00:36:53,797 --> 00:36:54,631
Of course.

482
00:36:55,591 --> 00:36:56,675
I'll drink to that.

483
00:37:25,704 --> 00:37:26,747
Down the hatch.

484
00:37:35,047 --> 00:37:36,297
Come on, man,

485
00:37:36,298 --> 00:37:37,883
it's the world's smallest glass!

486
00:38:05,118 --> 00:38:07,119
May I help you, Comrade?

487
00:38:07,120 --> 00:38:09,581
Alexander Volkov. State Security.
May I come in?

488
00:38:17,631 --> 00:38:19,591
You live alone here, I take it?

489
00:38:20,843 --> 00:38:21,843
Yes.

490
00:38:21,844 --> 00:38:23,095
What is this about?

491
00:38:23,887 --> 00:38:26,013
An aircraft went down
12 kilometers from here.

492
00:38:26,014 --> 00:38:29,977
We are asking local residents whether
they heard or saw anything of interest.

493
00:38:32,062 --> 00:38:33,605
No, sir.

494
00:38:34,648 --> 00:38:35,649
I see.

495
00:38:42,281 --> 00:38:44,032
My daughter, Anna.

496
00:38:45,367 --> 00:38:46,869
You were KGB?

497
00:38:47,995 --> 00:38:48,953
How…

498
00:38:48,954 --> 00:38:50,706
Your signet ring.

499
00:38:51,748 --> 00:38:53,458
Division?

500
00:38:54,459 --> 00:38:55,752
Alpha Group.

501
00:39:01,049 --> 00:39:02,341
I commend you for your service.

502
00:39:02,342 --> 00:39:03,886
What made you leave?

503
00:39:05,053 --> 00:39:07,014
My mind was becoming less sharp.

504
00:39:09,933 --> 00:39:12,144
You strike me as having a very sharp mind.

505
00:39:25,741 --> 00:39:27,326
What is down here?

506
00:39:28,202 --> 00:39:29,369
Root cellar.

507
00:39:29,995 --> 00:39:31,496
Humor me.

508
00:39:32,915 --> 00:39:35,375
There is nothing.

509
00:39:41,298 --> 00:39:42,633
Very well.

510
00:39:54,102 --> 00:39:55,562
I've caught you!

511
00:40:00,150 --> 00:40:01,192
Samogon?

512
00:40:01,193 --> 00:40:02,360
Yes…

513
00:40:02,361 --> 00:40:05,697
You do know that home distilling
of spirits is against the law?

514
00:40:06,198 --> 00:40:08,574
I'm sorry, Comrade Volkov. I didn't know--

515
00:40:08,575 --> 00:40:09,618
Please.

516
00:40:10,410 --> 00:40:11,994
I will make you a deal.

517
00:40:11,995 --> 00:40:13,704
You share a glass with me

518
00:40:13,705 --> 00:40:15,332
and it will be our secret.

519
00:40:17,000 --> 00:40:19,044
Of course.

520
00:40:40,148 --> 00:40:41,357
You can taste the beets.

521
00:40:41,358 --> 00:40:42,943
Thank you very much.

522
00:40:44,570 --> 00:40:48,574
Reminds me of the moonshine
we had in Prague in '68.

523
00:40:49,074 --> 00:40:51,201
I was a lowly junior lieutenant.

524
00:40:51,869 --> 00:40:55,872
We spent two months in the barracks
with very little to do.

525
00:40:55,873 --> 00:40:57,082
So we drank.

526
00:40:59,001 --> 00:41:01,295
You must miss it.

527
00:41:02,337 --> 00:41:03,589
The camaraderie.

528
00:41:04,173 --> 00:41:07,384
The sacrifices we make for our motherland.

529
00:41:09,428 --> 00:41:11,972
I think about it every day.

530
00:41:15,392 --> 00:41:16,476
Well,

531
00:41:17,227 --> 00:41:18,812
thank you for your hospitality.

532
00:41:20,522 --> 00:41:22,148
I must continue my survey.

533
00:41:22,149 --> 00:41:23,650
Certainly.

534
00:41:24,234 --> 00:41:28,864
I trust I can find you here
should we need to speak again?

535
00:41:30,532 --> 00:41:32,450
If anything occurs to you,

536
00:41:32,451 --> 00:41:33,994
do not hesitate to call.

537
00:41:35,495 --> 00:41:37,414
I wish you continued good health.

538
00:41:38,165 --> 00:41:39,458
You and your daughter.

539
00:41:49,343 --> 00:41:50,344
Christ.

540
00:41:51,970 --> 00:41:53,804
Thought you were gonna ask him
to stay for dinner.

541
00:41:53,805 --> 00:41:55,723
- This is bad.
- What do you mean? He left.

542
00:41:55,724 --> 00:41:58,059
He will speak to the men at the bar.
They will identify us.

543
00:41:58,060 --> 00:41:59,644
We must leave immediately.

544
00:41:59,645 --> 00:42:02,230
Well, I'll tell ya something.
That Samogon stings.

545
00:42:02,231 --> 00:42:03,565
Front and back, Nick.

546
00:42:12,783 --> 00:42:14,493
I don't know if it's safe
to drive that way.

547
00:42:15,035 --> 00:42:16,827
There's no time for safety.

548
00:42:16,828 --> 00:42:19,248
I'm getting a lot of your beard
in my mouth.

549
00:42:20,541 --> 00:42:23,709
Okay. Can we just, maybe…
maybe just put your hands on the wheel

550
00:42:23,710 --> 00:42:24,794
or slow down a little?

551
00:42:24,795 --> 00:42:27,046
We don't know whether they have
connected us yet.

552
00:42:27,047 --> 00:42:28,506
We need to change our appearance.

553
00:42:28,507 --> 00:42:30,968
Well, if we hit a tree,
that'll change our appearance.

554
00:42:40,018 --> 00:42:42,103
How long is the drive to Lake Vozhe?

555
00:42:42,104 --> 00:42:43,271
Three days.

556
00:42:43,272 --> 00:42:45,399
But we will get there in one.

557
00:42:46,191 --> 00:42:47,192
How's that?

558
00:42:49,736 --> 00:42:51,613
- We're not driving.
- What're you doing?

559
00:42:52,155 --> 00:42:53,448
They'll be looking for my truck.

560
00:42:54,032 --> 00:42:55,033
So?

561
00:42:55,826 --> 00:42:57,034
You'll want to get out.

562
00:42:57,035 --> 00:42:58,871
What? Why?

563
00:43:01,331 --> 00:43:02,332
Yep.

564
00:43:06,378 --> 00:43:07,588
Is this a lake?

565
00:43:08,422 --> 00:43:10,506
You could've told me
you were gonna do that.

566
00:43:10,507 --> 00:43:13,551
I apologize. I assumed you knew
standard tracking evasion.

567
00:43:13,552 --> 00:43:15,761
Do they not train pilots in such methods?

568
00:43:15,762 --> 00:43:19,224
No, Nick. No, they don't train us
to hide our planes in frozen lakes.

569
00:43:20,392 --> 00:43:21,351
Where are we going?

570
00:43:21,977 --> 00:43:24,271
Railroad station. Through here.

571
00:43:25,898 --> 00:43:28,400
Somehow, your beard is
still attacking my mouth.

572
00:43:34,114 --> 00:43:39,870
Our divisions are searching
the north and eastern edges of the forest.

573
00:43:40,913 --> 00:43:44,166
The second pilot landed here.

574
00:43:46,793 --> 00:43:48,211
No body?

575
00:43:48,212 --> 00:43:49,629
He survived.

576
00:43:49,630 --> 00:43:51,507
The snow has covered his tracks.

577
00:44:02,267 --> 00:44:03,351
His boot.

578
00:44:03,352 --> 00:44:05,811
We are waiting for
equipment to retrieve it.

579
00:44:05,812 --> 00:44:06,980
Hold this.

580
00:44:43,183 --> 00:44:44,810
Find his photograph.

581
00:44:46,186 --> 00:44:47,896
Get it in front of all security personnel.

582
00:45:06,123 --> 00:45:08,709
Couldn't have shot us down
closer to the border?

583
00:45:10,252 --> 00:45:11,336
"Us"?

584
00:45:12,045 --> 00:45:13,838
Yeah, I had a copilot, Sanders. He…

585
00:45:13,839 --> 00:45:15,090
He didn't make it.

586
00:45:15,591 --> 00:45:16,592
I'm sorry.

587
00:45:17,593 --> 00:45:20,344
He was an asshole, but he deserved better.

588
00:45:20,345 --> 00:45:23,724
It takes great courage
to be a fighter pilot.

589
00:45:25,976 --> 00:45:27,227
I suppose so, yeah.

590
00:45:28,353 --> 00:45:29,855
It's all I ever wanted to do.

591
00:45:31,398 --> 00:45:35,819
Actually, I wanted to build ships
for the Navy like my dad.

592
00:45:37,404 --> 00:45:41,991
Then when I was eight,
he took me onboard the USS <i>Randolph</i>

593
00:45:41,992 --> 00:45:44,494
to watch the F-9s land for CQ…

594
00:45:46,788 --> 00:45:49,624
and the way those guys
walked across the flight deck

595
00:45:49,625 --> 00:45:53,629
with their helmets in their hands,
and everybody, including my dad,

596
00:45:55,297 --> 00:45:57,299
looked at them like they were gods,
and then…

597
00:45:57,799 --> 00:46:00,469
then that was it. I was… I was hooked.

598
00:46:02,596 --> 00:46:04,306
So you wanted to be God.

599
00:46:07,559 --> 00:46:10,436
I wanted to serve my country.
I think you can understand that.

600
00:46:10,437 --> 00:46:12,480
Fear drove me to serve mine.

601
00:46:12,481 --> 00:46:14,858
We were told that
America would destroy us.

602
00:46:15,442 --> 00:46:18,736
That your greed and soullessness
would bring the end of humanity.

603
00:46:18,737 --> 00:46:21,489
And we were taught that you were cogs
in a giant world-killing machine.

604
00:46:21,490 --> 00:46:23,449
We were both fed lies.

605
00:46:23,450 --> 00:46:24,700
I wasn't fed lies.

606
00:46:24,701 --> 00:46:27,203
It was Khrushchev that said,
"We will bury you."

607
00:46:27,204 --> 00:46:30,456
And Reagan called us the evil empire.
Do you believe we are all evil?

608
00:46:30,457 --> 00:46:31,707
I believe you're brainwashed.

609
00:46:31,708 --> 00:46:33,501
Products of the system
that you grew up in.

610
00:46:33,502 --> 00:46:36,713
But you are not
a product of your system, Troy?

611
00:46:41,593 --> 00:46:43,220
Don't you KGB me.

612
00:46:46,473 --> 00:46:48,183
I'm going to rest my eyes.

613
00:46:49,351 --> 00:46:52,229
Please wake me when
we get to Starokostyantynivske.

614
00:46:53,146 --> 00:46:55,482
I don't see any reason why
I won't be able to do that.

615
00:47:40,819 --> 00:47:43,405
Nick. Wake up.

616
00:47:48,952 --> 00:47:50,954
I think we need to change cars.

617
00:47:59,505 --> 00:48:00,672
Now I've got herpes.

618
00:48:18,649 --> 00:48:20,609
Nick, I really need you to wake up.

619
00:48:26,073 --> 00:48:27,407
Don't look in the folder.

620
00:48:31,078 --> 00:48:32,955
Yeah, he's showing his friends.

621
00:48:56,979 --> 00:48:58,062
Nice nap?

622
00:48:58,063 --> 00:49:00,440
Comrades, I am an officer of the KGB.

623
00:49:01,024 --> 00:49:04,319
I am delivering this prisoner
to my superiors.

624
00:49:05,863 --> 00:49:06,946
Why should we believe you?

625
00:49:06,947 --> 00:49:08,948
Why would I lie to my brothers-in-arms?

626
00:49:08,949 --> 00:49:10,950
Listen to me right now!

627
00:49:10,951 --> 00:49:12,451
I'm doing this to convey the impression

628
00:49:12,452 --> 00:49:13,911
- that you are my prisoner.
- Okay.

629
00:49:13,912 --> 00:49:16,874
But this ruse will not last for long.

630
00:49:20,627 --> 00:49:22,546
In 30 seconds,
this train will enter a tunnel.

631
00:49:23,172 --> 00:49:25,257
There will be a moment before
the carriage lights come on.

632
00:49:26,008 --> 00:49:27,091
Hey!

633
00:49:27,092 --> 00:49:28,551
I am so sorry.

634
00:49:28,552 --> 00:49:29,886
What the hell?

635
00:49:29,887 --> 00:49:32,306
In that moment,
we run toward the front of the train.

636
00:49:33,098 --> 00:49:34,182
Comrade.

637
00:49:34,183 --> 00:49:37,643
These credentials expired ten years ago.

638
00:49:37,644 --> 00:49:38,603
Did they?

639
00:49:38,604 --> 00:49:40,147
I am taking you both into custody.

640
00:49:41,773 --> 00:49:42,983
Why?

641
00:49:46,695 --> 00:49:47,612
There!

642
00:49:47,613 --> 00:49:49,072
Stop them!

643
00:49:55,245 --> 00:49:57,748
What'd you put us on?
A goddamn troop train?

644
00:50:29,655 --> 00:50:31,030
We must jump.

645
00:50:31,031 --> 00:50:34,701
All right. On three. Two. One.

646
00:50:36,537 --> 00:50:37,538
Jesus.

647
00:50:40,666 --> 00:50:43,710
- We're running out of train.
- As long as we run out of tunnel first.

648
00:51:04,481 --> 00:51:05,816
We're almost out.

649
00:51:21,874 --> 00:51:24,334
Maybe we should've waited
till the Cold War is over.

650
00:51:33,468 --> 00:51:35,596
We need to find shelter for the night.

651
00:51:36,096 --> 00:51:37,847
Think there's a Red Roof Inn here?

652
00:51:37,848 --> 00:51:39,349
I don't know what that is.

653
00:51:40,350 --> 00:51:41,976
Where are you going?

654
00:51:41,977 --> 00:51:43,145
I'm finding shelter.

655
00:51:46,440 --> 00:51:47,690
Do you know these people?

656
00:51:47,691 --> 00:51:48,692
No. Why would I?

657
00:52:03,207 --> 00:52:05,833
- What did you say to her?
- That we are cold and hungry.

658
00:52:05,834 --> 00:52:07,460
And she's just gonna let us in?

659
00:52:07,461 --> 00:52:09,546
And you said we were all evil.

660
00:52:19,932 --> 00:52:20,848
Vodka.

661
00:52:20,849 --> 00:52:22,391
- That--
- She says, "Please enjoy."

662
00:52:22,392 --> 00:52:24,227
Okay, thank-- No, thank you.

663
00:52:24,228 --> 00:52:25,311
Okay.

664
00:52:25,312 --> 00:52:28,523
Are you sure that this is a good idea,
bringing an American in here?

665
00:52:28,524 --> 00:52:30,024
I told them you were my cousin.

666
00:52:30,025 --> 00:52:34,362
What if they've seen my picture on TV?
They could call the authorities.

667
00:52:34,363 --> 00:52:35,697
You see a television here?

668
00:52:36,573 --> 00:52:38,992
- It could be in the other room.
- Do you see another room?

669
00:52:41,620 --> 00:52:43,204
- No, I do not see…
- Please.

670
00:52:43,205 --> 00:52:45,040
…another room. Okay. Thank you. All right.

671
00:52:45,624 --> 00:52:47,167
This looks delicious.

672
00:52:55,300 --> 00:52:57,135
What is with the kid?

673
00:52:58,053 --> 00:52:59,679
I'm guessing these
aren't his parents, right?

674
00:52:59,680 --> 00:53:01,598
Although, that would explain a lot.

675
00:53:08,981 --> 00:53:10,064
His name is Gorgo.

676
00:53:10,065 --> 00:53:12,525
His parents had to move
to Moscow to find work.

677
00:53:12,526 --> 00:53:14,902
So, now he stays here
with his grandparents.

678
00:53:14,903 --> 00:53:18,031
Oh, hey. Hey, Gorgo. What's up?

679
00:53:20,325 --> 00:53:23,245
Okay. Just gonna pull that away.

680
00:53:31,879 --> 00:53:34,547
His parents send money back here,
but every day it is a struggle.

681
00:53:34,548 --> 00:53:38,551
Sometimes they must decide between
coal for the stove or food for the table.

682
00:53:38,552 --> 00:53:40,304
Yeah?

683
00:53:40,846 --> 00:53:42,598
Well, then why… why would they let us in?

684
00:53:44,183 --> 00:53:47,852
If someone came to the door
in America and asked for help,

685
00:53:47,853 --> 00:53:49,396
wouldn't they be taken in?

686
00:53:50,731 --> 00:53:51,899
Of course they would.

687
00:53:56,195 --> 00:53:57,196
Thank you.

688
00:54:05,913 --> 00:54:08,789
It's interesting.
They taught you so many ways to fight us,

689
00:54:08,790 --> 00:54:11,710
but you've never learned
the first thing about us.

690
00:54:13,629 --> 00:54:16,048
Do you know the cormorant bird?

691
00:54:16,840 --> 00:54:19,759
You are… What?
You are all over the map tonight.

692
00:54:19,760 --> 00:54:23,095
When hunting fish, the cormorant will dive
60 feet below the sea

693
00:54:23,096 --> 00:54:25,348
and remain underwater for over a minute.

694
00:54:25,349 --> 00:54:27,683
Out of its element. Completely vulnerable.

695
00:54:27,684 --> 00:54:31,230
But the only way for the bird
to understand the fish

696
00:54:31,939 --> 00:54:33,524
is by swimming with them.

697
00:54:36,818 --> 00:54:38,111
Just gonna…

698
00:54:48,539 --> 00:54:50,290
Are we all sleeping in the same room?

699
00:55:10,978 --> 00:55:13,272
Apparently, Lake Vozhe
is just down this road.

700
00:55:16,191 --> 00:55:17,442
Hey. Hey…

701
00:55:19,444 --> 00:55:21,487
Look, that's… That's…

702
00:55:21,488 --> 00:55:23,699
That's for you. You take that.

703
00:55:25,242 --> 00:55:27,034
No, no, that's the least I can do.

704
00:55:27,035 --> 00:55:28,787
You take it.

705
00:55:33,041 --> 00:55:34,625
Okay. All right, thank you.

706
00:55:34,626 --> 00:55:35,877
Okay.

707
00:55:35,878 --> 00:55:37,461
That felt good. All right.

708
00:55:37,462 --> 00:55:39,464
Let's not make the missus jealous.
All right.

709
00:55:40,632 --> 00:55:41,633
Get out of here.

710
00:55:43,802 --> 00:55:45,429
That was a very nice gesture.

711
00:55:46,054 --> 00:55:47,973
It's worth more than
he would make in a year.

712
00:55:48,891 --> 00:55:52,811
Maybe they can finally afford to get
little Gorgo a proper cage with a lock.

713
00:55:57,608 --> 00:56:00,027
You know, I feel sorry for him
in many ways.

714
00:56:00,944 --> 00:56:02,028
For Gorgo?

715
00:56:02,029 --> 00:56:03,030
Yes, Gorgo.

716
00:56:03,572 --> 00:56:05,616
- He's a demon, Nick.
- He's unusual.

717
00:56:06,325 --> 00:56:07,951
Looked a demon right in the eyes.

718
00:56:17,753 --> 00:56:20,297
Yeah, those are
not private security. They're KGB.

719
00:56:21,423 --> 00:56:23,592
Only an apparatchik would
have such protection.

720
00:56:25,093 --> 00:56:26,469
So whoever lives in this dacha

721
00:56:26,470 --> 00:56:28,763
- has an Altai phone?
- More than likely.

722
00:56:28,764 --> 00:56:31,849
There's no car in the driveway. Meaning
our man is in town, but not at home.

723
00:56:31,850 --> 00:56:32,892
Good timing.

724
00:56:32,893 --> 00:56:34,310
So how are you gonna get past these guys?

725
00:56:34,311 --> 00:56:36,646
They're walking
a standard <i>vos'merka</i> patrol pattern.

726
00:56:36,647 --> 00:56:39,316
We simply wait for
the <i>seredina</i> gap to present itself.

727
00:56:41,485 --> 00:56:43,445
- So I'll follow you?
- You will follow me.

728
00:56:47,991 --> 00:56:49,158
What are you doing?

729
00:56:49,159 --> 00:56:50,494
Looking for their channel.

730
00:56:51,411 --> 00:56:52,788
Yeah, here it is.

731
00:56:58,585 --> 00:56:59,837
Go. Go, go.

732
00:57:03,465 --> 00:57:05,466
You learn that trick in the KGB?

733
00:57:05,467 --> 00:57:08,469
No. Young Pioneers. Like your Boy Scouts.

734
00:57:08,470 --> 00:57:13,015
Well, the Scouts taught me how to build
a very slow car out of a block of wood.

735
00:57:13,016 --> 00:57:14,183
If that comes in handy.

736
00:57:14,184 --> 00:57:15,185
It won't.

737
00:57:18,480 --> 00:57:19,648
Young Pioneers again?

738
00:57:20,524 --> 00:57:21,567
KGB.

739
00:57:28,615 --> 00:57:31,075
Okay, you search upstairs.
Stay in contact.

740
00:57:31,076 --> 00:57:32,077
Roger that.

741
00:57:32,786 --> 00:57:34,871
Wait, hold on.
I don't know what this phone looks like.

742
00:57:34,872 --> 00:57:37,707
Small briefcase.
About the size of a human baby.

743
00:57:37,708 --> 00:57:38,709
Got it.

744
00:57:46,842 --> 00:57:48,719
And this is the one they chose to frame.

745
00:58:06,445 --> 00:58:07,446
Neat.

746
00:58:08,655 --> 00:58:09,823
Nick.

747
00:58:13,076 --> 00:58:14,660
Troy, I have the phone.

748
00:58:14,661 --> 00:58:17,581
Oh, good. We can use it
to call a therapist for this child.

749
00:58:19,625 --> 00:58:21,210
- Here.
- Oh, that's good.

750
00:58:21,710 --> 00:58:23,669
When we get a safe distance
from the house,

751
00:58:23,670 --> 00:58:24,963
then you can make your call.

752
00:58:41,396 --> 00:58:43,315
That is not his wife.

753
00:58:44,149 --> 00:58:45,150
Uh-huh.

754
00:58:46,818 --> 00:58:49,655
She is a sexual escort.

755
00:58:50,447 --> 00:58:52,658
I can see that. Thank you.

756
00:58:55,327 --> 00:58:58,121
I meant to ask
now that we've grown closer,

757
00:58:58,956 --> 00:59:01,458
will you tell me
why they call you Assassin?

758
00:59:02,751 --> 00:59:05,419
Really?
For real, you're asking me this now?

759
00:59:05,420 --> 00:59:06,463
I'm very curious.

760
00:59:07,297 --> 00:59:08,631
It's private.

761
00:59:08,632 --> 00:59:09,800
I thought we were closer.

762
00:59:10,759 --> 00:59:11,760
We're not.

763
00:59:16,723 --> 00:59:18,891
Oh, she is unclothing him.

764
00:59:18,892 --> 00:59:21,686
Okay. I… I don't need the play-by-play.

765
00:59:21,687 --> 00:59:24,021
- But thank you.
- Let us hope this is merely the foreplay

766
00:59:24,022 --> 00:59:26,107
and they move upstairs
for the act of intercourse.

767
00:59:26,108 --> 00:59:29,152
You are scorching my ear
with the heat of your breath.

768
00:59:34,700 --> 00:59:37,159
Shit! It's my family!

769
00:59:37,160 --> 00:59:38,536
You said they were coming tomorrow!

770
00:59:38,537 --> 00:59:39,538
Quickly!

771
00:59:41,915 --> 00:59:43,916
Not that way! She'll see you!

772
00:59:43,917 --> 00:59:45,001
In there!

773
00:59:45,002 --> 00:59:46,795
Oh, she is coming here.

774
00:59:58,557 --> 00:59:59,641
Who are you?

775
01:00:01,059 --> 01:00:02,352
Burglars.

776
01:00:05,856 --> 01:00:07,398
Nadia! What are you doing here?

777
01:00:07,399 --> 01:00:09,318
We came up early to avoid traffic.

778
01:00:09,818 --> 01:00:11,402
Are you not happy to see us?

779
01:00:11,403 --> 01:00:12,612
Of course I'm happy.

780
01:00:12,613 --> 01:00:15,448
I have such a headache. I need some wine.

781
01:00:15,449 --> 01:00:16,949
I will get it.

782
01:00:16,950 --> 01:00:17,993
You sit down.

783
01:00:19,578 --> 01:00:21,288
And now the man is coming.

784
01:00:43,936 --> 01:00:46,020
My head has been pounding
since this morning.

785
01:00:46,021 --> 01:00:48,189
Please. No more whining.
You sound like the children.

786
01:00:48,190 --> 01:00:49,398
I'll draw you a bath.

787
01:00:49,399 --> 01:00:50,483
Don't rush me.

788
01:00:50,484 --> 01:00:52,736
The car ride was so tiring today.

789
01:01:06,250 --> 01:01:08,752
- Yeah?
- There's a call for you, sir.

790
01:01:09,461 --> 01:01:10,462
At this hour?

791
01:01:11,338 --> 01:01:15,217
If he is who he says he is,
you're gonna want to take it.

792
01:01:17,052 --> 01:01:20,179
<i>This is Consul General Hennessy.
To whom am I speaking?</i>

793
01:01:20,180 --> 01:01:24,725
This is Lieutenant Troy Kelly.
US Navy service number E02-77-41.

794
01:01:24,726 --> 01:01:27,144
I'm behind enemy lines
and I need immediate evac.

795
01:01:27,145 --> 01:01:29,605
Hold on, Lieutenant.
This line might not be secure.

796
01:01:29,606 --> 01:01:32,024
I'm calling from
a Russian Altai radiotelephone.

797
01:01:32,025 --> 01:01:33,568
There's no way they can monitor this.

798
01:01:33,569 --> 01:01:36,445
- How the hell did you get that?
- <i>That's not important.</i>

799
01:01:36,446 --> 01:01:38,447
All right, look,
if I give you my approximate location,

800
01:01:38,448 --> 01:01:40,491
how quickly can you dispatch an S&R team?

801
01:01:40,492 --> 01:01:41,450
<i>Okay, hold on.</i>

802
01:01:41,451 --> 01:01:43,161
It's not that simple.

803
01:01:44,037 --> 01:01:45,580
You were in a black op.

804
01:01:45,581 --> 01:01:47,748
The US can't recognize
your presence in this country.

805
01:01:47,749 --> 01:01:51,544
<i>An evac on Russian soil would be
an admission that we engaged in espionage.</i>

806
01:01:51,545 --> 01:01:52,880
So what am I supposed to do?

807
01:01:54,423 --> 01:01:57,842
If you can make it across
the border or to our embassy in Moscow,

808
01:01:57,843 --> 01:01:59,135
we would have plenipotentiary.

809
01:01:59,136 --> 01:02:01,430
<i>We could get you home.
Do you have a vehicle?</i>

810
01:02:04,558 --> 01:02:05,559
Yes, I do.

811
01:02:06,768 --> 01:02:09,730
There's one more thing.
There's a Russian guy.

812
01:02:10,272 --> 01:02:11,355
He's been helping me out.

813
01:02:11,356 --> 01:02:15,651
His name is Nick… Nikolai Ustinov.
I promised him and his daughter asylum.

814
01:02:15,652 --> 01:02:17,361
I'll have to clear that
with the ambassador.

815
01:02:17,362 --> 01:02:19,698
Well, you do that.
We'll call you when we get to Moscow.

816
01:02:21,366 --> 01:02:22,992
Did they give you an extraction point?

817
01:02:22,993 --> 01:02:26,079
Yeah, the embassy in Moscow.
They want us to go to them.

818
01:02:26,705 --> 01:02:31,460
But you are an American hero.
Why would they not come get you?

819
01:02:32,711 --> 01:02:34,755
Well, I guess
there's bigger things at stake.

820
01:02:36,507 --> 01:02:38,675
Okay. We will have to
pick up my daughter first.

821
01:02:39,259 --> 01:02:41,261
I will page her and ask her address.

822
01:02:41,762 --> 01:02:44,515
- You don't know where she lives?
- She moves around a lot.

823
01:02:45,807 --> 01:02:47,225
There is good news though.

824
01:02:47,226 --> 01:02:48,601
What's that?

825
01:02:48,602 --> 01:02:50,062
We have a cassette player.

826
01:02:51,480 --> 01:02:53,482
Twelve hours of that one tape.

827
01:02:56,944 --> 01:02:58,486
Who is the daddy in the song?

828
01:02:58,487 --> 01:03:00,614
You know,
I've always wondered that myself.

829
01:03:51,081 --> 01:03:53,499
It is too late
to get into the embassy tonight.

830
01:03:53,500 --> 01:03:55,127
We will stay with my daughter.

831
01:03:56,420 --> 01:03:57,962
If she allows us.

832
01:03:57,963 --> 01:03:59,881
Moscow always this angry?

833
01:03:59,882 --> 01:04:02,300
They're preparing for
the Mayday parade tomorrow.

834
01:04:02,301 --> 01:04:04,260
A celebration of Soviet might.

835
01:04:04,261 --> 01:04:06,930
The streets will be filled
with soldiers and weapons.

836
01:04:07,848 --> 01:04:10,350
In America, we just have a…
like a Snoopy balloon.

837
01:04:10,976 --> 01:04:12,644
Thanksgiving. So boring.

838
01:04:17,566 --> 01:04:19,985
Probably best to turn this off now.

839
01:04:22,696 --> 01:04:27,491
This is where Deputy Chairman
Dukhov's Altai phone was stolen.

840
01:04:27,492 --> 01:04:30,161
Just 90 kilometers south of here

841
01:04:30,162 --> 01:04:34,749
the railway
where our troops engaged with the targets.

842
01:04:34,750 --> 01:04:36,292
What time was the robbery?

843
01:04:36,293 --> 01:04:38,670
Approximately 2100 hours.

844
01:04:42,925 --> 01:04:47,554
Here. A call to the US Embassy at 2214
where monitoring was switched off.

845
01:04:48,138 --> 01:04:49,473
That must have been him.

846
01:04:59,566 --> 01:05:01,025
He is coming to us.

847
01:05:01,026 --> 01:05:03,069
I'll inform the general secretary.

848
01:05:03,070 --> 01:05:04,529
No.

849
01:05:04,530 --> 01:05:08,200
I want to greet
our American friend myself.

850
01:05:16,708 --> 01:05:18,210
This place is nice.

851
01:05:19,920 --> 01:05:21,255
Isn't nice.

852
01:05:33,642 --> 01:05:34,977
- What's that for?
- Follow me.

853
01:05:42,234 --> 01:05:43,234
You sure it's 709?

854
01:05:43,235 --> 01:05:44,778
It's 809. Come on, we'll take the stairs.

855
01:05:46,280 --> 01:05:47,573
Oh, come on.

856
01:05:49,533 --> 01:05:51,868
Hey, remember when you drove the car
onto the ice

857
01:05:51,869 --> 01:05:53,620
and then sank that car without warning me?

858
01:05:54,121 --> 01:05:55,497
Getting that same vibe here.

859
01:05:59,126 --> 01:06:00,127
Hello, Anna.

860
01:06:01,086 --> 01:06:02,629
Do you have a safe house we can use?

861
01:06:03,255 --> 01:06:04,173
Yes.

862
01:06:05,883 --> 01:06:06,884
Hi.

863
01:06:33,911 --> 01:06:35,494
All right, what is all this?

864
01:06:35,495 --> 01:06:38,789
There were four state security men
staking out the building.

865
01:06:38,790 --> 01:06:40,250
But they will not catch us.

866
01:06:44,463 --> 01:06:45,672
Eighth floor.

867
01:07:09,238 --> 01:07:10,739
Do you own or rent?

868
01:07:13,867 --> 01:07:15,118
Anna, come.

869
01:08:05,210 --> 01:08:06,627
I'm Troy, by the way.

870
01:08:06,628 --> 01:08:07,629
- Anna.
- Pleasure.

871
01:08:11,466 --> 01:08:12,967
So, first thing in the morning,

872
01:08:12,968 --> 01:08:15,011
Troy will call the embassy
on the Altai phone

873
01:08:15,012 --> 01:08:17,014
and the three of us
can leave here forever.

874
01:08:19,308 --> 01:08:21,143
Is this all true?
You're an Air Force pilot?

875
01:08:22,269 --> 01:08:23,769
No, Navy pilot.

876
01:08:23,770 --> 01:08:26,564
We have planes and boats.
It's confusing, I know.

877
01:08:28,317 --> 01:08:30,944
Do you know how long
it's been since we've spoken?

878
01:08:32,446 --> 01:08:34,155
Why would you think I'd go with you?

879
01:08:34,156 --> 01:08:36,407
It's a chance to start over.

880
01:08:36,408 --> 01:08:37,408
I'm not like you.

881
01:08:39,536 --> 01:08:42,038
I don't want to run off
to the woods and hide.

882
01:08:42,747 --> 01:08:44,831
I'd rather make the home I have better.

883
01:08:44,832 --> 01:08:46,542
You won't be safe if you stay.

884
01:08:47,377 --> 01:08:49,421
I've never been safe here.

885
01:08:50,714 --> 01:08:52,633
Go to the States if you can.

886
01:08:55,051 --> 01:08:58,054
You have many things to feel guilty about.

887
01:08:58,055 --> 01:09:00,307
But leaving me shouldn't be one of them.

888
01:09:06,438 --> 01:09:08,272
Does the stove work?

889
01:09:08,273 --> 01:09:10,441
What, are you gonna
blow this place up too?

890
01:09:10,442 --> 01:09:11,483
I'm making tea.

891
01:09:11,484 --> 01:09:12,944
Oh, I'll have some tea.

892
01:09:16,156 --> 01:09:18,283
You some kind of freedom fighter?

893
01:09:21,118 --> 01:09:23,913
The organization I'm in
works for human rights.

894
01:09:24,747 --> 01:09:25,832
They allow that here?

895
01:09:27,835 --> 01:09:29,919
They can only forbid what they know about.

896
01:09:29,920 --> 01:09:32,880
And they can only punish
the ones they catch.

897
01:09:32,881 --> 01:09:35,384
So they employ men like my father
to sniff us out.

898
01:09:37,803 --> 01:09:39,845
Did you do the stitching yourself?

899
01:09:39,846 --> 01:09:41,848
No. No, no, no, your dad did it.

900
01:09:41,849 --> 01:09:44,851
He's trained to create wounds,
not treat them.

901
01:09:44,852 --> 01:09:47,562
Appreciate you taking us in for the night.

902
01:09:47,563 --> 01:09:50,858
I didn't have much choice.
You led my father's comrades to my door.

903
01:09:51,774 --> 01:09:53,443
Well, ex-comrades.

904
01:09:55,195 --> 01:09:56,196
Yeah.

905
01:09:59,241 --> 01:10:01,285
I don't know what my father has told you,

906
01:10:02,703 --> 01:10:04,621
but I wouldn't put much trust in him.

907
01:10:05,497 --> 01:10:08,082
- Right.
- He lies for a living.

908
01:10:08,083 --> 01:10:09,792
I get that. But on the other hand,

909
01:10:09,793 --> 01:10:12,962
he's saved my life, like, 44 times
in the last couple of days,

910
01:10:12,963 --> 01:10:15,423
so I'm gonna give him
the benefit of the doubt.

911
01:10:15,424 --> 01:10:19,219
Yes. Well, I hope you make it home
safe to your family.

912
01:10:20,095 --> 01:10:21,889
No family, just my dad.

913
01:10:22,556 --> 01:10:26,100
Really?
A handsome American pilot like you?

914
01:10:26,101 --> 01:10:28,604
I assumed you had a wife and kids

915
01:10:29,521 --> 01:10:31,606
and a house with a picket fence.

916
01:10:31,607 --> 01:10:34,025
No. Single. Condo with a chain-link.

917
01:10:34,026 --> 01:10:36,528
I've been mostly focused on my work.

918
01:10:37,613 --> 01:10:40,032
Looks like that's really paying off.

919
01:10:40,782 --> 01:10:41,991
Thanks.

920
01:10:41,992 --> 01:10:44,578
What about you?
Is there a man in your life?

921
01:10:49,041 --> 01:10:50,042
Not my type.

922
01:10:50,626 --> 01:10:51,668
Come on, all men?

923
01:10:58,050 --> 01:11:02,386
I figure since I'm hiding you,
you can keep a secret.

924
01:11:02,387 --> 01:11:03,805
Does Nick know?

925
01:11:08,602 --> 01:11:11,188
It is against the law to be gay
in the Soviet Union.

926
01:11:11,688 --> 01:11:13,941
And that law is enforced by the KGB.

927
01:11:16,276 --> 01:11:17,277
Right.

928
01:11:23,784 --> 01:11:25,160
Anna's great, by the way.

929
01:11:26,787 --> 01:11:28,330
Much gentler touch than you.

930
01:11:29,081 --> 01:11:30,958
She's a very special girl.

931
01:11:32,751 --> 01:11:35,127
I wish things were different between us.

932
01:11:35,128 --> 01:11:36,672
Yeah, I bet she does too.

933
01:11:42,344 --> 01:11:44,096
Don't you…
Don't you see what you're doing?

934
01:11:45,681 --> 01:11:47,974
You're making the same mistakes
that Maverick did.

935
01:11:47,975 --> 01:11:49,141
Maverick from <i>Top Gun</i>?

936
01:11:49,142 --> 01:11:51,561
- Do you know another Maverick?
- Not really.

937
01:11:51,562 --> 01:11:52,645
After Goose died,

938
01:11:52,646 --> 01:11:56,357
Mav was wracked with guilt about all the
things that he could've done differently.

939
01:11:56,358 --> 01:12:00,487
All the things that he wished he could've
said to him. But it was too late.

940
01:12:01,905 --> 01:12:04,074
You still have a chance to talk to Anna.

941
01:12:05,117 --> 01:12:06,200
And who knows?

942
01:12:06,201 --> 01:12:10,454
You know, maybe you'll discover
stuff you didn't know about each other.

943
01:12:10,455 --> 01:12:11,539
What stuff?

944
01:12:11,540 --> 01:12:13,958
Yeah, well, private stuff.
You know, personal--

945
01:12:13,959 --> 01:12:15,711
You mean that she likes women?

946
01:12:16,795 --> 01:12:18,796
- Wait, you know?
- Yeah, I've always known.

947
01:12:18,797 --> 01:12:21,175
I mean, that's one of the reasons
why I left the KGB.

948
01:12:21,675 --> 01:12:24,720
How could I work for a system
that considered my own child a criminal?

949
01:12:25,554 --> 01:12:28,139
Why… Why didn't you talk to her about it?

950
01:12:28,140 --> 01:12:32,351
Look, if Anna wanted to discuss it,
she would come to me.

951
01:12:32,352 --> 01:12:33,686
That's not how it works.

952
01:12:33,687 --> 01:12:34,688
You're the dad.

953
01:12:35,314 --> 01:12:38,525
You need to show her that she
doesn't need to hide who she is from you.

954
01:12:41,778 --> 01:12:44,155
Are all fighter pilots
as sensitive as you?

955
01:12:44,156 --> 01:12:45,157
Yes.

956
01:12:50,787 --> 01:12:54,374
Can we talk?

957
01:12:56,793 --> 01:12:57,794
All right.

958
01:14:38,687 --> 01:14:41,522
DAD, CALL ME
WHEN YOU MAKE IT TO AMERICA.

959
01:14:41,523 --> 01:14:44,067
AND DON'T WORRY ABOUT ME.

960
01:14:49,031 --> 01:14:51,699
<i>Our building
is typically surveilled by state security,</i>

961
01:14:51,700 --> 01:14:54,243
<i>so arrive at 9:00
and come straight to the gate.</i>

962
01:14:54,244 --> 01:14:57,205
<i>You'll debrief here and ship out
to Ramstein before end of day.</i>

963
01:14:57,206 --> 01:14:58,789
Hooyah. Thank you, sir.

964
01:14:58,790 --> 01:15:00,374
<i>Don't hang up yet, Lieutenant.</i>

965
01:15:00,375 --> 01:15:01,960
I spoke with the ambassador.

966
01:15:03,045 --> 01:15:06,589
Unfortunately, we cannot
offer asylum to your civilian escort.

967
01:15:06,590 --> 01:15:08,592
Hold on. You don't understand.

968
01:15:09,092 --> 01:15:13,304
I wouldn't be here if not for Nick.
I promised I'd get him to the States.

969
01:15:13,305 --> 01:15:17,350
I hear you, but Nikolai Ustinov
is no ordinary citizen.

970
01:15:17,351 --> 01:15:18,935
<i>Did you know he was KGB?</i>

971
01:15:18,936 --> 01:15:21,229
Yeah. Yeah, he told me. But he quit.

972
01:15:21,230 --> 01:15:24,857
This is a very delicate moment
in US-Soviet relations.

973
01:15:24,858 --> 01:15:27,985
We give safe harbor to a KGB agent,
current or former--

974
01:15:27,986 --> 01:15:29,487
You gotta be kidding me.

975
01:15:29,488 --> 01:15:31,572
This guy is a major asset.

976
01:15:31,573 --> 01:15:34,033
A window into Soviet ops.
You should be jumping at this.

977
01:15:34,034 --> 01:15:37,036
My hands are tied.
This is coming from the highest level.

978
01:15:37,037 --> 01:15:39,039
Well, what am I supposed to tell the guy?

979
01:15:39,540 --> 01:15:42,084
He put his trust in me. In America.

980
01:15:42,918 --> 01:15:45,169
If I leave him behind,
we're no different than them.

981
01:15:45,170 --> 01:15:46,588
<i>This is bullshit.</i>

982
01:15:47,589 --> 01:15:49,967
I need confirmation
you'll be here at 9:00 this morning.

983
01:15:54,847 --> 01:15:55,848
I'll be there.

984
01:16:06,400 --> 01:16:07,609
It won't be long now.

985
01:16:10,404 --> 01:16:12,948
Listen, Nick, before we get there…

986
01:16:13,740 --> 01:16:17,326
I… When I talked to the Embassy,
I got some bad news and…

987
01:16:17,327 --> 01:16:20,539
Oh, is this… Is this a dead end? What--

988
01:16:26,503 --> 01:16:27,628
What is this?

989
01:16:27,629 --> 01:16:29,214
I thought we were friends.

990
01:16:30,340 --> 01:16:31,632
Nick, you ratted me out?

991
01:16:31,633 --> 01:16:34,136
You were leaving me
and my daughter to the wolves.

992
01:16:35,929 --> 01:16:37,848
Oh, come on, Nick. No, no, no, no, Nick.

993
01:16:39,766 --> 01:16:40,767
What did you do?

994
01:16:41,268 --> 01:16:44,229
Get off me! Hey! Hey, Nick!

995
01:17:04,625 --> 01:17:07,461
This was not our agreement!

996
01:17:14,384 --> 01:17:16,720
Get Volkov. This is unacceptable.

997
01:17:17,554 --> 01:17:18,639
Well, look who's here.

998
01:17:19,973 --> 01:17:21,766
Feeling pretty good about
making that call now?

999
01:17:21,767 --> 01:17:23,935
You lying bastard.
You promised you'd get me out of here.

1000
01:17:23,936 --> 01:17:26,562
I was gonna convince them
to take you in when we got to the Embassy.

1001
01:17:26,563 --> 01:17:29,106
More lies! Why didn't you tell me
they'd refused me asylum?

1002
01:17:29,107 --> 01:17:30,650
You never would've driven me there.

1003
01:17:30,651 --> 01:17:31,943
See? You're…

1004
01:17:31,944 --> 01:17:34,654
I swear to God
I was not gonna just leave you there.

1005
01:17:34,655 --> 01:17:36,198
I am… That is not who I am.

1006
01:17:37,032 --> 01:17:38,574
I don't know who you are.

1007
01:17:38,575 --> 01:17:41,328
Yes, you do. I'm a <i>Top Gun</i>.
I'm Maverick. You love me.

1008
01:17:42,704 --> 01:17:45,040
That is just a movie. I know that now.

1009
01:17:47,167 --> 01:17:48,126
Nick…

1010
01:17:51,046 --> 01:17:52,172
Gentlemen.

1011
01:17:53,048 --> 01:17:54,842
You lied. You said I'd be released.

1012
01:17:55,467 --> 01:17:58,345
And you will. One of you will.

1013
01:17:59,179 --> 01:18:00,931
- What is this?
- A confession.

1014
01:18:01,682 --> 01:18:03,600
It states that your objective here

1015
01:18:04,142 --> 01:18:07,521
was to infiltrate the Kremlin
and assassinate President Gorbachev.

1016
01:18:08,021 --> 01:18:11,024
And yours affirms
that he disclosed this mission to you.

1017
01:18:12,276 --> 01:18:13,693
Well, that's not true.

1018
01:18:13,694 --> 01:18:18,115
But if enough people believe it,
that will put an end to this Glasnost.

1019
01:18:19,616 --> 01:18:20,742
Openness.

1020
01:18:21,243 --> 01:18:24,663
The only thing we've opened ourselves to
is dissent and disorder.

1021
01:18:25,455 --> 01:18:28,375
Once you sign,
you'll be very well taken care of here.

1022
01:18:29,501 --> 01:18:33,379
A new identity with more
comfort and luxury than you can imagine.

1023
01:18:33,380 --> 01:18:38,050
You, sir, are an ugly man.
And that is an ugly suggestion.

1024
01:18:38,051 --> 01:18:41,387
Your country refused to extract you
from behind enemy lines,

1025
01:18:41,388 --> 01:18:44,183
- leaving you to fend for yourself.
- I know what I signed up for.

1026
01:18:45,559 --> 01:18:46,727
How about your father?

1027
01:18:48,145 --> 01:18:52,441
Did he know his government was killing him
and taking no responsibility?

1028
01:18:53,192 --> 01:18:54,734
What did you say?

1029
01:18:54,735 --> 01:18:56,320
We do our research.

1030
01:18:59,656 --> 01:19:00,949
Harold Kelly.

1031
01:19:01,617 --> 01:19:05,411
Diagnosed in 1983
with malignant pleural mesothelioma.

1032
01:19:05,412 --> 01:19:08,289
Worked for 32 years
at the San Diego shipyards

1033
01:19:08,290 --> 01:19:12,668
where he was exposed to high levels of
asbestos on a daily basis.

1034
01:19:12,669 --> 01:19:14,086
Why is this America's fault?

1035
01:19:14,087 --> 01:19:17,298
They knew the dangers, but suppressed
that information for decades

1036
01:19:17,299 --> 01:19:18,425
because it saved them money.

1037
01:19:19,009 --> 01:19:20,010
How would you know that?

1038
01:19:21,178 --> 01:19:24,430
This is a copy of the classified letter
from your Secretary of the Navy

1039
01:19:24,431 --> 01:19:26,974
dated September 1956.

1040
01:19:26,975 --> 01:19:28,852
I told you we do our research.

1041
01:19:30,062 --> 01:19:31,605
Nations are like people.

1042
01:19:32,898 --> 01:19:35,859
They do what they must to survive.

1043
01:19:36,735 --> 01:19:39,613
The only question is, will you?

1044
01:19:44,243 --> 01:19:45,244
I…

1045
01:19:46,578 --> 01:19:50,249
Before I came here, I…
I thought I knew what this country was.

1046
01:19:50,874 --> 01:19:52,876
But there's something I didn't know.

1047
01:19:53,794 --> 01:19:56,380
I didn't know that
there were good people here.

1048
01:19:57,214 --> 01:20:00,259
People who will risk their lives
to help a stranger.

1049
01:20:02,052 --> 01:20:04,888
So, no, I won't… I won't sign that.

1050
01:20:06,348 --> 01:20:09,351
Not for the sake of my country.
But for the sake of yours.

1051
01:20:12,396 --> 01:20:13,646
And you, comrade?

1052
01:20:13,647 --> 01:20:16,483
You've proven your loyalty
by turning him in.

1053
01:20:17,150 --> 01:20:20,404
Are you willing to take the final step
for your country?

1054
01:20:23,156 --> 01:20:24,616
For your family?

1055
01:20:30,789 --> 01:20:32,124
What is loyalty?

1056
01:20:32,791 --> 01:20:34,500
An exercise for fools.

1057
01:20:34,501 --> 01:20:36,628
America. Russia. It's all shit.

1058
01:20:37,421 --> 01:20:39,089
None of us escape what we are.

1059
01:20:40,465 --> 01:20:43,301
If it will protect me and my family,
I'll sign your paper.

1060
01:20:43,302 --> 01:20:44,303
Nick.

1061
01:20:45,095 --> 01:20:46,847
What difference does it make?

1062
01:20:47,598 --> 01:20:48,432
Very good.

1063
01:20:50,434 --> 01:20:52,436
Your KGB training taught you well.

1064
01:20:53,228 --> 01:20:58,066
My training also taught me
never to give a prisoner a sharp object.

1065
01:21:00,694 --> 01:21:02,612
Goddamn it. I knew it was an act.

1066
01:21:02,613 --> 01:21:04,864
- You did?
- No, but thank God.

1067
01:21:04,865 --> 01:21:07,658
- I still have not forgiven you.
- Same. Why do we need that?

1068
01:21:07,659 --> 01:21:10,162
If we survive,
this will make sure he does not.

1069
01:21:12,331 --> 01:21:14,124
- You ready for this?
- No.

1070
01:21:22,966 --> 01:21:24,133
Where are we?

1071
01:21:24,134 --> 01:21:28,679
KGB black site.
Used for less formal interrogations.

1072
01:21:28,680 --> 01:21:30,098
So Volkov was going rogue?

1073
01:21:30,891 --> 01:21:33,977
Some men have their own agendas.
But you would know that.

1074
01:21:34,811 --> 01:21:36,522
You're the one who
called the torture police.

1075
01:21:42,277 --> 01:21:43,278
Hey!

1076
01:21:58,669 --> 01:22:01,755
Stop! Stop!

1077
01:22:20,524 --> 01:22:23,276
- Do we go back to the Embassy?
- KGB will be swarming the area.

1078
01:22:23,277 --> 01:22:25,486
We need to leave the country.
Where's the nearest airport?

1079
01:22:25,487 --> 01:22:27,488
Sheremetyevo.
It's about 12 kilometers from here.

1080
01:22:27,489 --> 01:22:30,241
Okay, okay.
All right, well, we need a car.

1081
01:22:30,242 --> 01:22:32,369
A car they can stop.
We need something bigger.

1082
01:22:34,580 --> 01:22:36,622
- You wanna steal a tank?
- A tank they can shoot at.

1083
01:22:36,623 --> 01:22:38,292
They can shoot at anything.

1084
01:22:40,919 --> 01:22:41,920
Not anything.

1085
01:22:45,591 --> 01:22:46,674
No.

1086
01:22:46,675 --> 01:22:49,344
I doubt they'll shoot
at a nuclear missile.

1087
01:22:50,304 --> 01:22:51,972
- You doubt?
- Hey!

1088
01:23:14,953 --> 01:23:17,663
- What did you say to him?
- That he had driven over my baby.

1089
01:23:17,664 --> 01:23:21,126
That just popped into your head?
What is it with you and babies?

1090
01:23:23,212 --> 01:23:24,213
Hey!

1091
01:23:36,183 --> 01:23:37,309
Oh, God.

1092
01:23:42,356 --> 01:23:44,190
Get out of the car!

1093
01:23:44,191 --> 01:23:48,277
I want every unit in pursuit of
that mobile launcher.

1094
01:23:48,278 --> 01:23:50,447
No one is to fire on them!

1095
01:24:08,090 --> 01:24:10,342
- You're seeing this, right?
- I'm seeing it.

1096
01:24:38,579 --> 01:24:39,997
Bus, bus, bus, bus, bus, bus!

1097
01:25:12,112 --> 01:25:13,863
Maybe we should clear the air.

1098
01:25:13,864 --> 01:25:15,448
What is there to clear, Troy?

1099
01:25:15,449 --> 01:25:18,034
I risk everything to save you
and you pissed on my back.

1100
01:25:18,035 --> 01:25:21,830
Oh, that's funny 'cause I seem to
remember your piss running down my back.

1101
01:25:22,497 --> 01:25:26,083
I asked for asylum for you, they said no,
you threw me in a dungeon.

1102
01:25:26,084 --> 01:25:28,294
You promised
your country would protect us.

1103
01:25:28,295 --> 01:25:31,297
Well, it turns out that
my country isn't perfect.

1104
01:25:31,298 --> 01:25:34,051
My dad meant nothing to them
and neither do you or I.

1105
01:25:44,394 --> 01:25:45,687
Well, look, if I'm being…

1106
01:25:46,480 --> 01:25:48,565
if I'm being totally honest with myself,

1107
01:25:49,358 --> 01:25:51,235
maybe I was
just thinking about getting home.

1108
01:25:54,112 --> 01:25:56,073
Maybe I'm kind of a selfish guy.

1109
01:26:02,287 --> 01:26:05,249
The least I could've done was tell you
'cause you're my friend.

1110
01:26:06,333 --> 01:26:07,793
And that's what friends do.

1111
01:26:11,588 --> 01:26:12,798
Move.

1112
01:26:42,494 --> 01:26:44,746
Fine, you wanna know
why they call me Assassin?

1113
01:26:47,332 --> 01:26:50,459
Back when I was a JG,
I made my first carrier landing

1114
01:26:50,460 --> 01:26:51,461
and I was…

1115
01:26:52,337 --> 01:26:54,548
I was feeling pretty proud of myself.

1116
01:26:55,174 --> 01:26:56,592
So I get out of the plane,

1117
01:26:57,092 --> 01:26:59,052
I wasn't looking where I was going,

1118
01:27:00,387 --> 01:27:02,806
and I fell ass-first into a deck hatch.

1119
01:27:03,932 --> 01:27:05,600
A couple green shirts, they helped me up.

1120
01:27:05,601 --> 01:27:08,061
I look around to see
if anyone else noticed and I…

1121
01:27:09,563 --> 01:27:10,939
Shit, I did it again.

1122
01:27:12,149 --> 01:27:14,401
I fell ass-first into the same hatch.

1123
01:27:15,360 --> 01:27:16,486
I don't understand.

1124
01:27:16,987 --> 01:27:19,323
I fell, ass in.

1125
01:27:20,240 --> 01:27:21,742
Twice. Get it?

1126
01:27:22,326 --> 01:27:24,077
Ass, ass, in.

1127
01:27:27,748 --> 01:27:29,081
That is embarrassing.

1128
01:27:29,082 --> 01:27:33,295
Point is, I've never told
anyone outside the service that story.

1129
01:27:34,463 --> 01:27:36,507
But I'm telling you now as a gesture.

1130
01:27:38,050 --> 01:27:39,467
A gesture?

1131
01:27:39,468 --> 01:27:40,844
Of trust.

1132
01:27:42,596 --> 01:27:45,557
You're trusting that I will tell no one
that you disgraced yourself?

1133
01:27:46,767 --> 01:27:47,768
Yes.

1134
01:27:54,525 --> 01:27:56,150
Trying to pull another lever?

1135
01:27:56,151 --> 01:27:58,278
I appreciate you telling me your secret,

1136
01:27:58,946 --> 01:28:00,072
Ass-ass-in.

1137
01:28:03,700 --> 01:28:05,285
You can just run it together.

1138
01:28:07,246 --> 01:28:08,704
Ass-ass-in.

1139
01:28:08,705 --> 01:28:09,831
Assassin.

1140
01:28:10,415 --> 01:28:12,084
Our bomb's not gonna clear that.

1141
01:29:00,507 --> 01:29:03,593
- Where are the private jets?
- Private planes leave from Vnukovo.

1142
01:29:03,594 --> 01:29:04,678
Where's that?

1143
01:29:05,304 --> 01:29:06,722
An hour away.

1144
01:29:09,057 --> 01:29:10,809
Then I guess we're flying commercial.

1145
01:29:18,901 --> 01:29:22,112
My baby's in there!
My baby's in there!

1146
01:29:26,742 --> 01:29:28,409
Oh, God!

1147
01:29:28,410 --> 01:29:29,869
God!

1148
01:29:29,870 --> 01:29:30,786
Follow me!

1149
01:29:30,787 --> 01:29:32,914
- Why did you do that?
- What do you mean?

1150
01:29:32,915 --> 01:29:35,416
- I took a bullet for you.
- You didn't take it.

1151
01:29:35,417 --> 01:29:38,503
You shared it.
You can't stop a bullet with your hand.

1152
01:29:38,504 --> 01:29:40,589
I know that now. And I knew that then.

1153
01:29:52,935 --> 01:29:54,895
Pull up close to the cabin door!

1154
01:29:57,773 --> 01:29:58,774
Closer!

1155
01:29:59,691 --> 01:30:01,109
You wanna get the door, please?

1156
01:30:18,710 --> 01:30:20,420
What are they paying this guy?

1157
01:30:38,897 --> 01:30:39,731
Hey!

1158
01:31:38,582 --> 01:31:39,750
Once you are dead,

1159
01:31:40,417 --> 01:31:42,335
I will hunt down your daughter

1160
01:31:42,336 --> 01:31:43,795
and kill her too!

1161
01:31:47,299 --> 01:31:49,426
You are a traitor and a fool!

1162
01:31:55,098 --> 01:31:56,724
No, Volkov.

1163
01:31:56,725 --> 01:31:58,727
I must break you.

1164
01:31:59,353 --> 01:32:00,771
Is that what he said?

1165
01:32:09,238 --> 01:32:11,072
I don't think that killed him.

1166
01:32:11,073 --> 01:32:12,865
Who is flying the plane?

1167
01:32:12,866 --> 01:32:14,535
I put it on autopilot.

1168
01:32:15,619 --> 01:32:17,037
I put it on something.

1169
01:32:24,711 --> 01:32:26,213
How far until Finland?

1170
01:32:26,839 --> 01:32:27,840
Too far.

1171
01:32:28,507 --> 01:32:30,466
When we lost that engine,
we lost our airspeed.

1172
01:32:30,467 --> 01:32:33,052
We won't make it to Finland.
We don't have the fuel.

1173
01:32:33,053 --> 01:32:35,973
- Where does that leave us?
- Somewhere over the Baltic Sea.

1174
01:32:37,641 --> 01:32:39,517
Or if we can find Carl Vinson.

1175
01:32:39,518 --> 01:32:41,477
Who is this man, Vinson?
How can he help us?

1176
01:32:41,478 --> 01:32:43,355
He's not a man. He was a man.

1177
01:32:44,022 --> 01:32:45,356
Now he's an aircraft carrier.

1178
01:32:45,357 --> 01:32:47,150
Can a plane this size land on a carrier?

1179
01:32:47,985 --> 01:32:49,862
Technically, no. But physically,

1180
01:32:50,571 --> 01:32:51,572
probably not.

1181
01:32:54,992 --> 01:32:59,120
Captain, TAO.
Unidentified aircraft, course 110,

1182
01:32:59,121 --> 01:33:00,621
20 miles, 5,000 feet.

1183
01:33:00,622 --> 01:33:02,164
- Military?
- <i>No, sir.</i>

1184
01:33:02,165 --> 01:33:04,584
- <i>It's squawking commercial air.</i>
- All right, hail them on guard.

1185
01:33:04,585 --> 01:33:07,128
Unidentified aircraft, course 285,

1186
01:33:07,129 --> 01:33:10,006
<i>you are approaching a US warship
operating in international waters.</i>

1187
01:33:10,007 --> 01:33:12,091
<i>Request you identify yourself
and state intentions.</i>

1188
01:33:12,092 --> 01:33:15,261
This is Lieutenant Troy Kelly
of the United States Navy.

1189
01:33:15,262 --> 01:33:19,182
We are declaring an emergency
and requesting a ready deck upon arrival.

1190
01:33:19,183 --> 01:33:22,351
Unidentified aircraft,
alter course immediately or you

1191
01:33:22,352 --> 01:33:23,978
<i>may be subject to defensive measures.</i>

1192
01:33:23,979 --> 01:33:25,062
- Holy shit.
- What?

1193
01:33:25,063 --> 01:33:27,441
Okay, we can hear them
but they can't hear us.

1194
01:33:28,609 --> 01:33:31,194
Let's get a visual. Launch the alert five.

1195
01:33:31,195 --> 01:33:34,113
If we can't communicate with them,
they're gonna assume that we're hostile,

1196
01:33:34,114 --> 01:33:35,489
and they're gonna run an intercept on us.

1197
01:33:35,490 --> 01:33:38,910
No, I can fix this. How long do we have?

1198
01:33:38,911 --> 01:33:40,913
As long as you need,
until we're shot down.

1199
01:33:48,712 --> 01:33:51,714
<i>Skopa Air, alter course
immediately and acknowledge.</i>

1200
01:33:51,715 --> 01:33:54,551
- How is that coming?
- It is trickier than my ham radio.

1201
01:33:55,427 --> 01:33:57,762
Captain, that was an alert from Interpol.

1202
01:33:57,763 --> 01:34:01,349
Commercial ATR 42 was seized by gunmen
at Sheremetyevo an hour ago.

1203
01:34:01,350 --> 01:34:04,394
TAO, set weapons posture one, all mounts.

1204
01:34:16,323 --> 01:34:18,157
- They're detaching.
- That's good.

1205
01:34:18,158 --> 01:34:19,993
That's bad.
They're getting out of the way.

1206
01:34:25,207 --> 01:34:27,542
Designating track 141 hostile.

1207
01:34:27,543 --> 01:34:28,584
All mounts in remote.

1208
01:34:28,585 --> 01:34:29,919
Awaiting batteries release.

1209
01:34:29,920 --> 01:34:32,880
- Permission grant--
- <i>Repeat, this is Lieutenant Troy Kelly…</i>

1210
01:34:32,881 --> 01:34:36,218
- Belay that.
- 7741. I've been MIA. Do you copy?

1211
01:34:40,097 --> 01:34:41,223
<i>Welcome back, Lieutenant.</i>

1212
01:34:43,600 --> 01:34:45,560
Mayday! Mayday! Mayday!

1213
01:34:45,561 --> 01:34:48,480
We're below minimum fuel.
Request emergency landing. Over.

1214
01:34:49,064 --> 01:34:50,857
In that thing? Without a tailhook?

1215
01:34:50,858 --> 01:34:52,233
He's nuts.

1216
01:34:52,234 --> 01:34:54,402
What's the wingspan on the ATR-42?

1217
01:34:54,403 --> 01:34:55,404
Eighty-one feet, sir.

1218
01:34:56,154 --> 01:34:58,490
It's tight, but it's possible.

1219
01:35:00,242 --> 01:35:01,325
This is the captain.

1220
01:35:01,326 --> 01:35:04,161
<i>On the flight deck,
emergency pull-forward.</i>

1221
01:35:04,162 --> 01:35:05,706
<i>All ahead full.</i>

1222
01:35:09,793 --> 01:35:12,421
<i>Skopa Air 1628, you are clear for landing.</i>

1223
01:35:14,339 --> 01:35:16,300
Congratulations, Troy.

1224
01:35:18,218 --> 01:35:19,887
You're going home.

1225
01:35:21,180 --> 01:35:22,598
Yes, I am.

1226
01:35:36,653 --> 01:35:39,156
Captain, I need you to help me
with something before I can land.

1227
01:35:39,698 --> 01:35:40,615
<i>Name it.</i>

1228
01:35:40,616 --> 01:35:45,912
I need permanent asylum status
for a Soviet citizen, Nikolai Ustinov.

1229
01:35:45,913 --> 01:35:46,829
What?

1230
01:35:46,830 --> 01:35:49,081
<i>I'm not authorized to grant that.</i>

1231
01:35:49,082 --> 01:35:52,043
Then I think you should ask
somebody at the State Department who can,

1232
01:35:52,044 --> 01:35:53,294
because you hear this?

1233
01:35:53,295 --> 01:35:55,505
<i>We're down to 20 kilos of fuel.</i>

1234
01:35:55,506 --> 01:35:57,965
I don't understand.
You've been given permission to land.

1235
01:35:57,966 --> 01:36:01,844
And I respectfully decline permission
to land until I get what I'm asking.

1236
01:36:01,845 --> 01:36:03,137
Fifteen kilos.

1237
01:36:03,138 --> 01:36:04,555
This guy's a soup sandwich.

1238
01:36:04,556 --> 01:36:06,057
He's also an American MIA.

1239
01:36:06,058 --> 01:36:08,351
Get Assistant Director Meadows
on the horn.

1240
01:36:08,352 --> 01:36:09,186
Quickly.

1241
01:36:10,521 --> 01:36:12,271
Why are you doing this?

1242
01:36:12,272 --> 01:36:16,068
'Cause right now the only way to stand up
for my country is to stand up to it.

1243
01:36:20,197 --> 01:36:21,490
Thank you.

1244
01:36:25,827 --> 01:36:27,538
We're dead-stick, Captain.

1245
01:36:37,673 --> 01:36:40,842
<i>Kelly, this is Karen Meadows
at the State Department.</i>

1246
01:36:40,843 --> 01:36:43,970
Consul General Hennessy
has apprised me of your situation.

1247
01:36:43,971 --> 01:36:46,848
<i>You should know
I don't like to have my arm twisted,</i>

1248
01:36:46,849 --> 01:36:49,100
<i>especially at 5:00 in the morning.</i>

1249
01:36:49,101 --> 01:36:50,643
Understood, ma'am. Sorry to wake you.

1250
01:36:50,644 --> 01:36:52,478
<i>But considering the circumstances,</i>

1251
01:36:52,479 --> 01:36:55,440
I am officially granting
your request for asylum.

1252
01:36:56,066 --> 01:36:58,610
<i>And I wanna thank you
for your service, Lieutenant.</i>

1253
01:36:59,611 --> 01:37:01,655
Now, land the goddamn plane.

1254
01:37:03,532 --> 01:37:04,992
You risked your life for me.

1255
01:37:06,034 --> 01:37:08,579
Sort of. There's a reserve fuel tank.

1256
01:37:29,057 --> 01:37:30,474
Flaps 30.

1257
01:37:30,475 --> 01:37:33,395
Condition levers max. On-slope, on center.

1258
01:37:33,937 --> 01:37:36,648
It's like I'm inside my favorite movie.

1259
01:38:10,098 --> 01:38:11,892
- Hey, Troy.
- Yeah?

1260
01:38:12,893 --> 01:38:14,894
You can be my wingman any--

1261
01:38:14,895 --> 01:38:16,647
No. No, no, no. What is that? No.

1262
01:38:17,147 --> 01:38:18,440
No. We're not gonna do that.

1263
01:38:19,024 --> 01:38:20,150
I understand.

1264
01:38:27,699 --> 01:38:30,369
It's been my great honor
to serve this nation

1265
01:38:31,370 --> 01:38:35,457
and uphold the Navy's motto,
<i>non sibi sed patriae.</i>

1266
01:38:37,000 --> 01:38:38,669
"Not for self but for country."

1267
01:38:40,420 --> 01:38:42,004
Which is why I have the utmost confidence

1268
01:38:42,005 --> 01:38:44,757
that this body will issue
a public acknowledgement

1269
01:38:44,758 --> 01:38:49,054
of the government's use of cancer-causing
materials in military construction.

1270
01:38:55,561 --> 01:38:59,230
Additionally, I'd ask for the
establishment of a compensation committee

1271
01:38:59,231 --> 01:39:01,650
to reimburse the good people,
including my father,

1272
01:39:02,234 --> 01:39:05,362
who sacrificed their own well-being
to safeguard ours.

1273
01:39:08,615 --> 01:39:09,824
Thank you, Lieutenant.

1274
01:39:09,825 --> 01:39:12,201
This country owes you a debt of gratitude

1275
01:39:12,202 --> 01:39:14,830
and we will take your request
under serious advisement.

1276
01:39:18,458 --> 01:39:19,584
Sorry.

1277
01:39:19,585 --> 01:39:22,795
It's a little trick I picked up
from the Russian boy scouts.

1278
01:39:22,796 --> 01:39:25,507
I probably should've mentioned
that I have this.

1279
01:39:26,258 --> 01:39:28,259
This is a copy of a classified letter

1280
01:39:28,260 --> 01:39:30,845
sent from
the Secretary of the Navy in 1956

1281
01:39:30,846 --> 01:39:35,851
{\an8}recognizing the health risks
posed by asbestos to shipyard workers.

1282
01:39:36,977 --> 01:39:39,604
I have the original somewhere. I just
can't quite remember where I put it.

1283
01:39:39,605 --> 01:39:42,191
This is just very damning though.
Very, very damning.

1284
01:39:43,817 --> 01:39:44,943
Bravo.

1285
01:39:49,990 --> 01:39:52,868
Well? How's the Big Mac?
Is it everything you hoped?

1286
01:39:53,493 --> 01:39:56,787
It tastes like America.

1287
01:39:56,788 --> 01:39:59,248
Huh. When do you head out?

1288
01:39:59,249 --> 01:40:00,166
At dawn.

1289
01:40:00,167 --> 01:40:02,460
I want to have lunch at the Liberty Bell.

1290
01:40:02,461 --> 01:40:04,337
Well, that's everyone's dream.

1291
01:40:04,338 --> 01:40:05,421
It's a beaut.

1292
01:40:05,422 --> 01:40:06,590
It is.

1293
01:40:07,758 --> 01:40:10,511
Didn't you say that
you had months of leave saved up?

1294
01:40:11,136 --> 01:40:14,388
After my little stunt, they may
put me on permanent leave.

1295
01:40:14,389 --> 01:40:16,516
So, why don't you join me?

1296
01:40:16,517 --> 01:40:18,893
You know, the kitchen table
folds down into a second bed.

1297
01:40:18,894 --> 01:40:21,896
There's a car phone,
so I can keep in touch with Anna.

1298
01:40:21,897 --> 01:40:23,356
I have a cassette player.

1299
01:40:23,357 --> 01:40:25,441
- I'm out.
- Understood.

1300
01:40:25,442 --> 01:40:27,318
But isn't it time
that you explored the country

1301
01:40:27,319 --> 01:40:29,154
that you fought so hard to protect?

1302
01:40:31,907 --> 01:40:33,199
I don't know.

1303
01:40:33,200 --> 01:40:35,911
Come on, Lieutenant.

1304
01:40:37,996 --> 01:40:39,414
One more adventure.

1305
01:40:42,209 --> 01:40:44,294
Let me finish my drink before I decide.

1306
01:40:44,878 --> 01:40:45,921
Of course.

1307
01:41:01,812 --> 01:41:02,980
I think you are fucking with me.
